WEBVTT

1
00:00:46.046 --> 00:00:47.889
XFM, 104. 9.

2
00:00:48.006 --> 00:00:50.634
I'm Ricky Gervais. With me Steve Merchant.

3
00:00:50.759 --> 00:00:53.228
I'll tell you what. I was walking here today,

4
00:00:53.345 --> 00:00:56.394
and the West End is crammed.

5
00:00:56.515 --> 00:00:59.109
There's helicopters. There's police.

6
00:00:59.226 --> 00:01:02.355
There's about a million people
sort of just milling around,

7
00:01:02.479 --> 00:01:04.277
standing around with placards and stuff.

8
00:01:04.398 --> 00:01:07.368
They've got too much time
on their hands. They need a war.

9
00:01:07.484 --> 00:01:10.033
You don't read the newspapers, do you?

10
00:01:10.153 --> 00:01:11.746
Boring.

11
00:01:11.863 --> 00:01:14.491
BBC Radio Four.

12
00:01:14.616 --> 00:01:17.791
What's thought to be the biggest
demonstration in British history

13
00:01:17.911 --> 00:01:19.254
is taking place in Central London.

14
00:01:19.371 --> 00:01:21.624
The organizers say nearly two million people

15
00:01:21.748 --> 00:01:25.002
have gathered to oppose war against Iraq.

16
00:01:25.127 --> 00:01:28.131
From Auckland to Athens, Berlin to Bangkok,

17
00:01:28.255 --> 00:01:31.134
some 600 cities across the world.

18
00:01:32.301 --> 00:01:33.644
Can you do that again?

19
00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:37.310
15th of February 2003.

20
00:01:37.431 --> 00:01:40.401
We're in London,
just outside Trafalgar Square.

21
00:01:40.517 --> 00:01:42.235
It's the anti-war march.

22
00:01:42.352 --> 00:01:45.401
There's lots and lots of people here.

23
00:01:47.232 --> 00:01:49.234
It's a terrible legacy...

24
00:01:49.359 --> 00:01:52.659
...for those people who died on 9/11,

25
00:01:52.779 --> 00:01:55.202
that we're now killing
more people in their name.

26
00:01:55.324 --> 00:01:57.668
That's my opinion, anyway.

27
00:01:58.827 --> 00:02:04.300
February 15th was the single largest
mobilization of people

28
00:02:04.416 --> 00:02:07.420
in the history of humanity, bar none.

29
00:02:09.504 --> 00:02:11.882
It was the biggest demonstration coordinated

30
00:02:12.007 --> 00:02:14.135
in the history of the whole Earth.

31
00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:18.438
Do you think we can stop this war?

32
00:02:18.555 --> 00:02:20.603
I think if we tried really hard, yeah.

33
00:02:20.724 --> 00:02:23.318
I think we can stop this war.

34
00:02:25.812 --> 00:02:29.112
The Prime Minister
avoided the protestors outside

35
00:02:29.232 --> 00:02:31.610
chanting "No blood for oil."

36
00:02:31.735 --> 00:02:34.739
Millions of British people telling you
we don't want this war.

37
00:02:34.863 --> 00:02:36.365
Now will you listen?

38
00:02:36.490 --> 00:02:39.960
Listen to the people, Tony.
Listen to the people.

39
00:02:40.077 --> 00:02:44.583
This is a battle with only one outcome.

40
00:02:44.706 --> 00:02:47.084
Our victory, not theirs.

41
00:02:48.460 --> 00:02:53.887
Something began on that day
that, uh... that cannot be reversed.

42
00:03:04.309 --> 00:03:05.356
Whoo!

43
00:04:26.433 --> 00:04:28.606
I was in New York. I'm a New Yorker.

44
00:04:30.353 --> 00:04:33.323
I work as a nurse practitioner,
and I was at work that day.

45
00:04:38.028 --> 00:04:41.828
I was at work at Tinker
Air Force Base in Oklahoma.

46
00:04:41.948 --> 00:04:43.700
It was an ordinary day.

47
00:04:46.703 --> 00:04:51.049
I was in an airplane on my way
from Stockholm to New York.

48
00:04:51.166 --> 00:04:55.171
And I remember waking up
in the morning, early, jetlagged,

49
00:04:55.295 --> 00:05:00.552
and I went out jogging,
and I came in and turned on the news.

50
00:05:01.968 --> 00:05:05.689
I'm going to interrupt
because of the latest news.

51
00:05:05.806 --> 00:05:08.730
A plane has crashed
into the World Trade Center.

52
00:05:08.850 --> 00:05:10.773
We've got Warner on the phone,

53
00:05:10.894 --> 00:05:12.567
who's watching and looking at it right now.

54
00:05:12.687 --> 00:05:15.782
You can see the fire, you can see the smoke.

55
00:05:15.899 --> 00:05:19.028
I mean, it's a monster hole, you know.

56
00:05:20.070 --> 00:05:21.242
Oh, my God.

57
00:05:23.615 --> 00:05:25.492
That looks like a second plane.

58
00:05:27.786 --> 00:05:29.459
Oh, my God.

59
00:05:29.579 --> 00:05:34.676
The second tower has exploded
in a gargantuan explosion!

60
00:05:35.919 --> 00:05:39.640
And at that point I didn't know
that my brother was at the Trade Center.

61
00:05:43.760 --> 00:05:45.603
It was horrific.

62
00:05:54.646 --> 00:05:57.775
And I knew, I knew in a flash,

63
00:05:57.899 --> 00:06:00.152
whatever was happening at that moment,

64
00:06:00.277 --> 00:06:03.156
this was the beginning
of something even worse.

65
00:06:10.287 --> 00:06:13.040
The deliberate and deadly attacks

66
00:06:13.164 --> 00:06:16.384
which were carried out yesterday
against our country

67
00:06:16.501 --> 00:06:18.674
were more than acts of terror.

68
00:06:18.795 --> 00:06:20.468
They were acts of war.

69
00:06:20.589 --> 00:06:25.140
September 12th 2001,
was the day that changed the world.

70
00:06:25.260 --> 00:06:26.807
Not September 11th.

71
00:06:26.928 --> 00:06:31.354
Because that was the day that
George W Bush announced to the world

72
00:06:31.474 --> 00:06:36.071
that the answer to this huge crime
would be our war.

73
00:06:36.187 --> 00:06:37.689
It was downhill after that.

74
00:06:37.814 --> 00:06:41.034
The evidence now shows that on the very...

75
00:06:41.151 --> 00:06:43.370
Well, within 24 hours of the attack,

76
00:06:43.486 --> 00:06:46.285
they had basically decided to attack Iraq.

77
00:06:46.406 --> 00:06:47.908
Was it Cheney or Rumsfeld, one of those,

78
00:06:48.033 --> 00:06:50.877
at the first meeting
after the attack on the Twin Towers,

79
00:06:50.994 --> 00:06:52.621
said, "That's it, let's go for Iraq?"

80
00:06:52.746 --> 00:06:55.841
And now's an opportunity

81
00:06:55.957 --> 00:07:00.007
to do, uh, generations a favor

82
00:07:00.128 --> 00:07:04.133
by coming together and whipping terrorism.

83
00:07:04.257 --> 00:07:06.510
It is a long-term campaign

84
00:07:06.635 --> 00:07:09.514
which is why we are characterizing it
as a war.

85
00:07:09.638 --> 00:07:11.606
Your loss we count as our loss.

86
00:07:12.724 --> 00:07:15.227
Your struggle we take as our struggle.

87
00:07:15.352 --> 00:07:18.105
Now that war has been declared on us,

88
00:07:18.229 --> 00:07:22.154
we will lead the world
to victory, to victory.

89
00:07:22.275 --> 00:07:25.870
This is a moment to seize.

90
00:07:25.987 --> 00:07:29.457
The kaleidoscope has been shaken.
The pieces are in flux.

91
00:07:29.574 --> 00:07:32.123
Soon they will settle again.

92
00:07:32.243 --> 00:07:36.965
Before they do,
let us reorder this world around us.

93
00:07:42.295 --> 00:07:44.389
We were fearful. We were concerned.

94
00:07:44.506 --> 00:07:49.353
We were anxious about the way
that the US was going to retaliate,

95
00:07:49.469 --> 00:07:52.268
and we had very little confidence
they were gonna behave sensibly.

96
00:07:54.391 --> 00:07:57.645
Over a few days,
we started talking on the phone,

97
00:07:57.769 --> 00:07:59.737
getting together, and said "Look,

98
00:07:59.854 --> 00:08:04.075
we're gonna set up a Stop The War Coalition."

99
00:08:04.192 --> 00:08:06.115
They set up the Stop The War Coalition.

100
00:08:06.236 --> 00:08:09.206
They asked me to be president of it,
which I very happily accepted.

101
00:08:09.322 --> 00:08:12.201
And there was a meeting called.

102
00:08:12.325 --> 00:08:14.874
They knew immediately
that there was going to be a war,

103
00:08:14.995 --> 00:08:18.465
and so they had a meeting
in Friends House here.

104
00:08:18.581 --> 00:08:22.461
I attended that meeting. It was amazing.

105
00:08:22.585 --> 00:08:27.637
Hall after hall after hall.
The entire building was taken over.

106
00:08:29.217 --> 00:08:31.766
It took just five days to organize,

107
00:08:31.886 --> 00:08:36.312
but the Stop The War protest
and meeting attracted so many people,

108
00:08:36.433 --> 00:08:38.810
they had to close the doors.

109
00:08:41.104 --> 00:08:42.697
I wrote a leaflet for that meeting,

110
00:08:42.814 --> 00:08:45.567
and the title of the meeting
was "Stop The War Before It Starts."

111
00:08:45.692 --> 00:08:47.114
That's what was on the leaflet.

112
00:08:47.235 --> 00:08:49.954
And that's why it became
the Stop The War Coalition.

113
00:08:51.573 --> 00:08:54.122
It's just absolutely unbelievable

114
00:08:54.242 --> 00:08:59.624
that that particular event
triggered such a momentous movement.

115
00:09:03.043 --> 00:09:07.389
The war on terrorism begins.
America and Britain strike Afghanistan.

116
00:09:07.505 --> 00:09:10.475
President Bush says the campaign
will be sustained,

117
00:09:10.592 --> 00:09:12.720
comprehensive and relentless.

118
00:09:14.471 --> 00:09:17.566
Afghanistan, I was completely gung-ho for it.

119
00:09:20.435 --> 00:09:22.654
We were excited to be doing our job

120
00:09:22.771 --> 00:09:26.617
and exacting retribution
on the enemy for 9/11.

121
00:09:28.318 --> 00:09:31.822
And in fact, I even had pictures
of myself on a ladder

122
00:09:31.946 --> 00:09:35.166
in the bomb bay of a B-1 bomber
signing bombs.

123
00:09:37.702 --> 00:09:39.704
Various obscenities

124
00:09:39.829 --> 00:09:44.005
and things like "This is for my sister
in New York City."

125
00:09:47.712 --> 00:09:51.717
It became clear that phase one
was going to be Afghanistan,

126
00:09:51.841 --> 00:09:55.141
and, of course, it wasn't going to take
much to defeat the Taliban.

127
00:09:55.261 --> 00:09:59.687
It wasn't much of a war, and it wasn't said,

128
00:09:59.808 --> 00:10:02.982
but the sense was there wasn't
much glory in it, either.

129
00:10:04.604 --> 00:10:08.359
It didn't somehow answer 9/11,

130
00:10:08.483 --> 00:10:12.704
and that's when the talk of phase two began.

131
00:10:12.821 --> 00:10:16.951
My fellow Americans, let's roll.

132
00:10:24.666 --> 00:10:32.642
Bush came up with his extraordinary
speech in January 2002.

133
00:10:32.757 --> 00:10:36.307
Our war against terror is only beginning.

134
00:10:36.427 --> 00:10:40.148
Iraq continues to flaunt
its hostility toward America

135
00:10:40.265 --> 00:10:41.733
and to support terror.

136
00:10:41.850 --> 00:10:45.775
States like these and their terrorist allies

137
00:10:45.895 --> 00:10:48.819
constitute an axis of evil.

138
00:10:53.528 --> 00:10:56.077
We wanted connection with 9/11,

139
00:10:56.197 --> 00:10:58.871
and we pressed that and pressed that
and pressed that

140
00:10:58.992 --> 00:11:01.586
until we lied to the American people.

141
00:11:01.703 --> 00:11:05.003
I mean, we had something like 51 to 60
percent of the American people

142
00:11:05.123 --> 00:11:09.594
believing that Saddam Hussein
was connected with 9/11.

143
00:11:12.589 --> 00:11:16.059
From then on we're building up
towards a war with Iraq,

144
00:11:16.176 --> 00:11:21.808
and we now know how closely involved
the British Government was with this.

145
00:11:21.931 --> 00:11:26.186
But to allow weapons of mass destruction
to be developed by a state like Iraq,

146
00:11:26.311 --> 00:11:29.064
without let or hindrance,

147
00:11:29.189 --> 00:11:33.114
would be grossly to ignore
the lessons of September 11th,

148
00:11:33.234 --> 00:11:35.032
and we will not do it.

149
00:11:35.153 --> 00:11:38.248
We were sharing intelligence.
We were sharing preparations

150
00:11:38.364 --> 00:11:43.495
for the kind of marketing campaign
we'd do if we decided to go to war.

151
00:11:53.755 --> 00:11:55.302
The weapons of mass destruction

152
00:11:55.423 --> 00:11:58.302
was an important thing
to hold up to the public,

153
00:11:58.426 --> 00:12:02.101
and I'm not saying
that they were in bad faith,

154
00:12:02.222 --> 00:12:05.066
but they had many other reasons
that pushed them about,

155
00:12:05.183 --> 00:12:07.311
and about which they didn't talk.

156
00:12:14.609 --> 00:12:17.453
8:00. The first element
of the government's strategy

157
00:12:17.570 --> 00:12:19.698
for winning over the doubters was released.

158
00:12:19.822 --> 00:12:23.122
MPs had three and a half hours
to read the dossier.

159
00:12:24.702 --> 00:12:26.625
This dossier is based on the work

160
00:12:26.746 --> 00:12:28.965
of the British Joint Intelligence Committee.

161
00:12:29.082 --> 00:12:34.088
It is extensive, detailed and authoritative.

162
00:12:34.212 --> 00:12:39.184
It concludes that Iraq has chemical
and biological weapons.

163
00:12:39.300 --> 00:12:42.804
That Saddam has continued to produce them.

164
00:12:42.929 --> 00:12:45.682
That he has existing
and active military plans

165
00:12:45.807 --> 00:12:48.731
for the use of chemical
and biological weapons,

166
00:12:48.851 --> 00:12:51.821
which could be activated within 45 minutes,

167
00:12:51.938 --> 00:12:55.067
including against his own Shia population.

168
00:12:55.191 --> 00:12:57.159
They were 100 percent sure

169
00:12:57.277 --> 00:12:59.200
that there were weapons of mass destruction.

170
00:12:59.320 --> 00:13:02.073
They had zero percent of knowledge
where they were.

171
00:13:02.198 --> 00:13:04.576
I felt I knew what had happened

172
00:13:04.701 --> 00:13:08.956
in the creation of an interior group
of knowing people.

173
00:13:09.080 --> 00:13:12.300
So what was going on
in the corridors of power was,

174
00:13:12.417 --> 00:13:13.839
"I've been there."

175
00:13:13.960 --> 00:13:16.304
"I've been to the Secret Service
headquarters."

176
00:13:16.421 --> 00:13:18.014
"I've seen the papers."

177
00:13:18.131 --> 00:13:20.225
"If you'd seen what I've seen,

178
00:13:20.341 --> 00:13:22.343
you'd know how to go
through those corridors."

179
00:13:22.468 --> 00:13:27.440
So there was the creation
of a bunch of insiders

180
00:13:27.557 --> 00:13:33.655
who were also, in a sense,
distributing the same lie.

181
00:13:33.771 --> 00:13:35.648
The intelligence was never there.

182
00:13:41.070 --> 00:13:42.947
I remember the dodgy dossier.

183
00:13:43.072 --> 00:13:45.416
I remember the front page
on the Evening Standard.

184
00:13:45.533 --> 00:13:47.160
I've got it somewhere in this room.

185
00:13:47.285 --> 00:13:49.413
I remember keeping it, you know,

186
00:13:49.537 --> 00:13:52.586
that Iraq could attack within
45 minutes or something.

187
00:13:52.707 --> 00:13:57.429
Those 45 minutes, I think,
was what really became famous

188
00:13:57.545 --> 00:14:01.891
because they wanted to create a fear.

189
00:14:02.008 --> 00:14:05.808
I remember hearing it on the news
and thinking, "45 minutes to attack?"

190
00:14:05.928 --> 00:14:08.852
"That... that's quite a peril."

191
00:14:08.973 --> 00:14:12.193
You just read that,
and that seemed pretty far-reaching,

192
00:14:12.310 --> 00:14:14.483
but you didn't know
what information they had.

193
00:14:14.604 --> 00:14:18.984
You don't want to disbelieve a Prime Minister

194
00:14:19.108 --> 00:14:21.076
when a Prime Minister puts out
a document like that

195
00:14:21.194 --> 00:14:22.571
from 10 Downing Street.

196
00:14:24.781 --> 00:14:29.127
I really wanted to find out
what the evidence was.

197
00:14:29.243 --> 00:14:33.123
So I wrote to the government
and I got a copy of the dossier.

198
00:14:34.874 --> 00:14:37.127
And I thought, "OK,
this is all very technical."

199
00:14:37.251 --> 00:14:38.878
"Can I check any of this?"

200
00:14:39.003 --> 00:14:42.758
So I found two weapons inspectors,

201
00:14:42.882 --> 00:14:47.729
and I asked them,
"How valid are these documents?"

202
00:14:47.845 --> 00:14:50.940
And they said,
"Well, we know this isn't true,

203
00:14:51.057 --> 00:14:53.401
we know that's not true,
we know this isn't true."

204
00:14:53.518 --> 00:14:54.940
Order!

205
00:14:55.061 --> 00:14:57.905
Now, it's amazing to me that I could do that,

206
00:14:58.022 --> 00:15:01.526
and yet none of the MPs
seemed to be able to do it.

207
00:15:01.651 --> 00:15:05.576
I think it also destroyed
Mr. Blair's credibility.

208
00:15:05.696 --> 00:15:10.623
He lost his credibility
in those 45 minutes, actually.

209
00:15:10.743 --> 00:15:12.541
It sort of completely caused me

210
00:15:12.662 --> 00:15:15.541
to become a different person politically...

211
00:15:16.707 --> 00:15:23.386
because I had to come to terms
with the fact, as a Conservative,

212
00:15:23.506 --> 00:15:28.228
that the institutions of the
British state had set out to tell lies.

213
00:15:28.344 --> 00:15:31.689
That is finally the bottom line.

214
00:15:31.806 --> 00:15:34.229
Lied to in order to go to war.

215
00:15:34.350 --> 00:15:35.852
There was a grand deception

216
00:15:35.977 --> 00:15:42.826
in which we all share
various amounts of, uh, responsibility

217
00:15:42.942 --> 00:15:49.541
and, uh, in which we, everybody,
didn't do their job.

218
00:15:51.701 --> 00:15:57.504
In the United States
the lies were told endlessly,

219
00:15:57.623 --> 00:16:02.174
and in Britain, the Murdoch Press,

220
00:16:02.295 --> 00:16:05.424
the Murdoch television networks,

221
00:16:05.548 --> 00:16:10.395
some parts of the BBC, all played the game.

222
00:16:10.511 --> 00:16:13.355
So, the military industrial complex is also,

223
00:16:13.473 --> 00:16:18.730
you know, has its analogue in the press,
the media industrial complex.

224
00:16:20.688 --> 00:16:24.568
America and our allies
are called once again...

225
00:16:26.027 --> 00:16:30.908
to defend the peace
against an aggressive tyrant.

226
00:16:31.032 --> 00:16:34.345
And we accept this responsibility.

227
00:16:37.121 --> 00:16:39.670
I remember saying...

228
00:16:39.790 --> 00:16:41.713
I don't know whether you can
say this on tape.

229
00:16:41.834 --> 00:16:45.304
But, "This bastard
is actually gonna take us to war."

230
00:16:45.421 --> 00:16:46.843
"This is not just rhetoric."

231
00:16:46.964 --> 00:16:49.638
I remember having these debates
a year before the invasion,

232
00:16:49.759 --> 00:16:51.386
and we'd say, "Oh, my God,

233
00:16:51.511 --> 00:16:53.479
it looks like
they're really going to invade Iraq,"

234
00:16:53.596 --> 00:16:54.688
and everyone would say,

235
00:16:54.805 --> 00:16:56.899
"Well, they're not that crazy.
It couldn't happen."

236
00:16:59.310 --> 00:17:02.940
US Central Command has been
moving aircraft carriers

237
00:17:03.064 --> 00:17:05.362
and war planes closer to Iraq.

238
00:17:05.483 --> 00:17:09.113
Now 600 of its specialists
are to move themselves

239
00:17:09.237 --> 00:17:12.707
to within a few hundred miles of Iraq.

240
00:17:12.823 --> 00:17:17.249
And so the global military enterprise
snaps into action.

241
00:17:19.163 --> 00:17:21.507
The public doesn't know about it.

242
00:17:21.624 --> 00:17:23.922
You can see pieces of it here and there.

243
00:17:25.711 --> 00:17:29.761
Units are being mobilized.
Tanks are loading on ships.

244
00:17:31.217 --> 00:17:34.061
The Marines are loading up.
You can see pieces of it.

245
00:17:36.681 --> 00:17:40.311
The bombing increased over Iraq
about 500 percent

246
00:17:40.434 --> 00:17:42.812
during the fall of 2002...

247
00:17:44.105 --> 00:17:46.449
with the purpose of trying
to goad Saddam Hussein

248
00:17:46.566 --> 00:17:49.536
into retaliating,
to give us a reason to go to war.

249
00:17:49.652 --> 00:17:52.326
And then I saw that. I was witness to that.

250
00:17:52.446 --> 00:17:55.040
But the President's still saying,
you know, "It may not..."

251
00:17:55.157 --> 00:17:56.784
"I hope it doesn't come to war."

252
00:17:56.909 --> 00:17:59.128
President Bush knows the real battle

253
00:17:59.245 --> 00:18:01.589
is to win support of the United Nations.

254
00:18:01.706 --> 00:18:04.550
When I saw Bush on TV saying,
"We're gonna try diplomacy first,"

255
00:18:04.667 --> 00:18:06.635
but then I saw the reality on the ground,

256
00:18:06.752 --> 00:18:10.882
something didn't sit right with me,
and I knew there was more to the story.

257
00:18:11.007 --> 00:18:16.355
On some level, globally,
people knew that this was not true,

258
00:18:16.470 --> 00:18:19.895
and I think that's what led to February 15th

259
00:18:20.016 --> 00:18:23.020
and the massive show on the streets

260
00:18:23.144 --> 00:18:27.490
was because people felt
like something's not right.

261
00:18:27.607 --> 00:18:31.453
Before our kids start coming home
from Iraq in body bags

262
00:18:31.569 --> 00:18:34.743
and women and children
start dying in Baghdad,

263
00:18:34.864 --> 00:18:39.040
I need to know, what did Iraq do to us?

264
00:18:39.160 --> 00:18:43.666
We had never raised money for an ad before,

265
00:18:43.789 --> 00:18:47.589
and we proposed this
"Let the Inspections Work" ad,

266
00:18:47.710 --> 00:18:49.758
sent it out to our members hoping to raise,

267
00:18:49.879 --> 00:18:51.756
you know, $20,000, $40,000.

268
00:18:51.881 --> 00:18:54.179
And we raised $400,000.

269
00:18:55.384 --> 00:18:57.182
And we made the ads,

270
00:18:57.303 --> 00:19:00.273
and we figured out everything
we could possibly do

271
00:19:00.389 --> 00:19:05.270
to build opposition to going to war.

272
00:19:07.146 --> 00:19:12.243
We organized demonstrations,
and it got bigger and bigger and bigger.

273
00:19:14.153 --> 00:19:16.076
Packing the streets to stop the war,

274
00:19:16.197 --> 00:19:18.575
the organizers of today's rally

275
00:19:18.699 --> 00:19:23.546
claim 300,000 people turned out
to show their anger over Iraq.

276
00:19:23.663 --> 00:19:26.633
I am in total opposition to the war.

277
00:19:26.749 --> 00:19:31.505
I think it's an obscenity,
and I think Blair is a scoundrel.

278
00:19:33.381 --> 00:19:35.930
The troop movements began to get underway,

279
00:19:36.050 --> 00:19:37.973
and that's when we really moved quickly

280
00:19:38.094 --> 00:19:40.938
to form the coalitions
in the US and internationally.

281
00:19:41.055 --> 00:19:45.526
It took a lot of courage for people
to raise their voices early on,

282
00:19:45.643 --> 00:19:50.319
prior to February 15th 2003.

283
00:19:53.526 --> 00:19:55.494
People were afraid to speak.

284
00:19:55.611 --> 00:19:58.285
They were afraid of being called unpatriotic.

285
00:19:58.406 --> 00:20:00.659
The Bush Administration and Saddam Hussein

286
00:20:00.783 --> 00:20:03.502
are not the only ones
cranking up the talk about war.

287
00:20:03.619 --> 00:20:07.089
So are American opponents of war with Iraq.

288
00:20:08.165 --> 00:20:11.886
We have a President
that is sending the ships,

289
00:20:12.002 --> 00:20:15.973
sending the war machines,
sending the bombs, sending the troops.

290
00:20:16.090 --> 00:20:18.809
And look what we have growing here at home.

291
00:20:18.926 --> 00:20:21.600
A mass, beautiful movement

292
00:20:21.721 --> 00:20:24.520
that's gonna stop them
from dropping those bombs.

293
00:20:24.640 --> 00:20:26.984
I'm becoming consumed with the thought

294
00:20:27.101 --> 00:20:29.820
that our country may soon be at war,

295
00:20:29.937 --> 00:20:32.531
waged partially in my brother's name

296
00:20:32.648 --> 00:20:37.324
and the names of thousands
killed last year in the terror attacks.

297
00:20:37.445 --> 00:20:39.038
We had to do something.

298
00:20:39.155 --> 00:20:42.375
To sit back and not do anything
was unbearable.

299
00:20:42.491 --> 00:20:47.338
We, as a civilized people,
must know better and do better.

300
00:20:47.455 --> 00:20:49.799
We must be a greater nation than that.

301
00:20:52.877 --> 00:20:55.050
You build confidence, and all
of a sudden you start daring

302
00:20:55.171 --> 00:20:59.267
to do stuff that you never ever thought
possible in the past.

303
00:20:59.383 --> 00:21:03.889
And the next step was to call
for a global day of demonstrations.

304
00:21:04.013 --> 00:21:06.186
I mean, I don't know
what we were thinking, actually,

305
00:21:06.307 --> 00:21:08.230
that there could be even
a global demonstration.

306
00:21:08.350 --> 00:21:10.023
I mean, nobody had ever done that.

307
00:21:10.144 --> 00:21:12.613
It was a, you know,
completely left-field idea

308
00:21:12.730 --> 00:21:14.198
even for us, and we'd had a few.

309
00:21:14.315 --> 00:21:19.572
And that day was put at 15th of February.

310
00:21:28.120 --> 00:21:31.499
So we started thinking together
what to do, what to do.

311
00:21:31.624 --> 00:21:37.381
We had a very strong European network.

312
00:21:39.089 --> 00:21:41.558
At the end of the European Social Forum,

313
00:21:41.675 --> 00:21:45.020
we had a massive assembly
in a disused railway station.

314
00:21:45.137 --> 00:21:49.108
There were thousands and thousands
of people packed into this meeting.

315
00:21:49.225 --> 00:21:53.696
We made the announcement
to say we have to make February 15th

316
00:21:53.813 --> 00:21:57.534
into the biggest day of global protest
there has ever been.

317
00:21:57.650 --> 00:22:03.123
I now hand over to my final speaker
on European coordination,

318
00:22:03.239 --> 00:22:04.911
Raffaella.

319
00:22:08.035 --> 00:22:11.460
To all citizens of Europe,

320
00:22:11.580 --> 00:22:16.051
we call on the movement
and the citizens of Europe

321
00:22:16.168 --> 00:22:18.387
to start from now

322
00:22:18.504 --> 00:22:23.931
organizing European anti-war demonstrations

323
00:22:24.051 --> 00:22:29.399
in every capital on February 15.

324
00:22:29.515 --> 00:22:32.314
We can stop this war.

325
00:22:37.189 --> 00:22:39.612
And the response was just overwhelming.

326
00:22:39.733 --> 00:22:41.201
It was absolutely tremendous.

327
00:22:41.318 --> 00:22:44.743
You need a moment
in which somebody says "Yes, we can,"

328
00:22:44.864 --> 00:22:46.741
and so you start.

329
00:22:46.866 --> 00:22:51.246
And after that, we knew that this day
was going to be something special,

330
00:22:51.370 --> 00:22:54.023
something the world had never seen before.

331
00:23:18.814 --> 00:23:21.363
The Stop The War Coalition
has been meeting in London

332
00:23:21.483 --> 00:23:24.987
to make plans for a demonstration
against war with Iraq.

333
00:23:25.112 --> 00:23:28.161
The London demonstration is due
to take place on February 15th

334
00:23:28.282 --> 00:23:32.287
to coincide with marches
in several other European capitals.

335
00:23:40.586 --> 00:23:42.554
Somebody said,
I don't even remember now who it was,

336
00:23:42.671 --> 00:23:45.470
that they had heard that folks in Europe
were planning

337
00:23:45.591 --> 00:23:50.597
for a day of international
demonstrations on February 15th

338
00:23:50.721 --> 00:23:54.476
against the war, and we said,
"Oh, OK, let's do that."

339
00:23:54.600 --> 00:23:57.570
The notion that we could pull this off
on a global level...

340
00:23:57.686 --> 00:24:02.066
Oh, my God, this was huge.
This was an enormous challenge.

341
00:24:02.191 --> 00:24:06.116
The notion that we were
part of something global

342
00:24:06.236 --> 00:24:09.911
was terrifying and thrilling
all at the same time.

343
00:24:13.744 --> 00:24:16.167
And Saddam Hussein must understand

344
00:24:16.288 --> 00:24:20.509
that if he does not disarm
for the sake of peace,

345
00:24:20.626 --> 00:24:25.052
we, along with others,
will go disarm Saddam Hussein.

346
00:24:25.172 --> 00:24:27.220
This is a matter of weeks, not months.

347
00:24:31.136 --> 00:24:33.184
It was only much later

348
00:24:33.305 --> 00:24:38.937
that I came across
the single most devastating document,

349
00:24:39.061 --> 00:24:44.443
which is the legal memorandum
written by Lord Goldsmith,

350
00:24:44.566 --> 00:24:48.787
in which Lord Goldsmith
tells the British Prime Minister,

351
00:24:48.904 --> 00:24:53.660
"You cannot use force without
a further Security Council resolution."

352
00:24:53.784 --> 00:24:59.006
If you go down the document,
at paragraph four,

353
00:24:59.123 --> 00:25:01.842
you've got Lord Goldsmith
telling the Prime Minister,

354
00:25:01.959 --> 00:25:03.802
"I remain of the view

355
00:25:03.919 --> 00:25:08.095
that the correct legal interpretation
of Resolution 1441

356
00:25:08.215 --> 00:25:11.890
is that it does not authorize
the use of military force

357
00:25:12.011 --> 00:25:14.981
without a further determination
by the Security Council."

358
00:25:15.097 --> 00:25:18.226
And just to the left of that,
a little scribble.

359
00:25:18.350 --> 00:25:20.569
"I just don't understand this."

360
00:25:20.686 --> 00:25:25.237
Who wrote that? Tony Blair wrote that.
And then the document was filed away.

361
00:25:25.357 --> 00:25:28.987
The timing of the document
is very significant.

362
00:25:29.111 --> 00:25:32.206
It was written and signed off
on 30th January.

363
00:25:32.322 --> 00:25:35.917
The next day, 31st January,

364
00:25:36.035 --> 00:25:40.211
the British Prime Minister met the
American President in the White House,

365
00:25:40.330 --> 00:25:46.963
and I've seen the internal
British note of the meeting

366
00:25:47.087 --> 00:25:50.057
prepared by Sir David Manning.

367
00:25:50.174 --> 00:25:53.269
And that note makes very clear,

368
00:25:53.385 --> 00:25:56.730
beyond any possible dispute, two things.

369
00:25:56.847 --> 00:26:01.648
First, by 31st January,
President Bush had decided

370
00:26:01.769 --> 00:26:05.273
that the war on Iraq would begin in March

371
00:26:05.397 --> 00:26:09.152
with or without a further
Security Council resolution.

372
00:26:09.276 --> 00:26:14.157
And second point,
Tony Blair told Bush at that meeting

373
00:26:14.281 --> 00:26:17.376
he was with him, whatever happened.

374
00:26:24.041 --> 00:26:27.090
I do remember the steady drumbeat to war.

375
00:26:27.211 --> 00:26:29.930
There was one sane voice in that crowd,

376
00:26:30.047 --> 00:26:34.223
and I remember talking to my dad
on the phone from Saudi Arabia

377
00:26:34.343 --> 00:26:37.313
and saying, you know,
Colin Powell is the only one

378
00:26:37.429 --> 00:26:39.102
that's gonna be able to stop this.

379
00:26:44.728 --> 00:26:47.527
It was the moment the world has waited for.

380
00:26:47.648 --> 00:26:52.529
America's best case against Iraq,
made by its top diplomat.

381
00:26:52.653 --> 00:26:55.406
When the Secretary finished
his dress rehearsal,

382
00:26:55.531 --> 00:26:58.751
the night before the presentation
to the Security Council,

383
00:26:58.867 --> 00:27:01.370
he looked at his watch. It was a little long.

384
00:27:01.495 --> 00:27:02.963
He looked at me,

385
00:27:03.080 --> 00:27:05.503
and he turned to Mr. Tenet,
the Director of the CIA,

386
00:27:05.624 --> 00:27:10.050
and he said, "George, you stand by everything

387
00:27:10.170 --> 00:27:13.299
that I just said, right?"

388
00:27:13.423 --> 00:27:18.224
And Mr. Tenet said, "I'm telling you,
what you just gave is solid."

389
00:27:18.345 --> 00:27:22.350
And he's telling the Secretary of State
it's a slam dunk.

390
00:27:22.474 --> 00:27:24.647
Saddam Hussein has weapons
of mass destruction.

391
00:27:24.768 --> 00:27:26.816
I mean, that's not the exact language

392
00:27:26.937 --> 00:27:29.861
George used with the Secretary,
but it was like that.

393
00:27:29.982 --> 00:27:32.326
And then the Secretary looked at him

394
00:27:32.442 --> 00:27:34.410
and got this smile on his face, and he said,

395
00:27:34.528 --> 00:27:36.781
"Well, George, you're gonna
be with me tomorrow."

396
00:27:36.905 --> 00:27:38.999
"You're gonna be in camera."

397
00:27:39.116 --> 00:27:40.914
That sort of surprised Mr. Tenet

398
00:27:41.034 --> 00:27:45.084
cos generally you don't put
the Director of the CIA on television.

399
00:27:45.205 --> 00:27:47.082
But you look at that film, and you will see

400
00:27:47.207 --> 00:27:49.801
that George Tenet is right
over the Secretary's shoulder.

401
00:27:49.918 --> 00:27:52.888
Saddam Hussein and his regime
have made no effort,

402
00:27:53.005 --> 00:27:58.557
no effort to disarm as required
by the international community.

403
00:27:58.677 --> 00:28:01.476
We have diagrammed what our sources reported

404
00:28:01.597 --> 00:28:03.144
about these mobile facilities.

405
00:28:03.265 --> 00:28:07.441
Colin Powell made his extraordinary
speech to the UN,

406
00:28:07.561 --> 00:28:09.234
in which there were sort of ice cream vans

407
00:28:09.354 --> 00:28:13.450
which we were told were
biological weapons things.

408
00:28:13.567 --> 00:28:16.320
They looked, actually, quite
like ice cream vans to me.

409
00:28:16.445 --> 00:28:18.493
They may well have been ice cream vans.

410
00:28:18.614 --> 00:28:21.083
What you're about to hear is a conversation

411
00:28:21.200 --> 00:28:22.952
that my government monitored.

412
00:28:23.076 --> 00:28:25.374
"We have this modified vehicle."

413
00:28:25.495 --> 00:28:28.169
"What do we say if one of them sees it?"

414
00:28:28.290 --> 00:28:30.338
His delivery was superb.

415
00:28:30.459 --> 00:28:33.633
He really should have won
an Academy Award for that performance.

416
00:28:33.754 --> 00:28:37.258
"I have one." "Which?"
"From where?" "From the workshop."

417
00:28:37.382 --> 00:28:39.680
"I'll come to see you in the morning."

418
00:28:39.801 --> 00:28:43.146
"I'm worried you all have something left."

419
00:28:44.598 --> 00:28:49.024
When I sat there in the Security Council
opposite Colin Powell

420
00:28:49.144 --> 00:28:51.363
and I heard these things,

421
00:28:51.480 --> 00:28:56.031
I kept a straight face, a poker face.

422
00:28:57.819 --> 00:28:59.321
But I was skeptical.

423
00:28:59.446 --> 00:29:01.323
And unless we act,

424
00:29:01.448 --> 00:29:05.669
we are confronting
an even more frightening future.

425
00:29:05.786 --> 00:29:08.539
He did not know that we were
perpetrating a hoax,

426
00:29:08.664 --> 00:29:11.258
but that is in effect what we were doing.

427
00:29:11.375 --> 00:29:15.596
I mean, we were telling the Security Council,

428
00:29:15.712 --> 00:29:18.807
people in the international community
and Americans,

429
00:29:18.924 --> 00:29:21.928
we were telling them all that Saddam
Hussein had weapons of mass destruction.

430
00:29:22.052 --> 00:29:24.100
He did not. He did not.

431
00:29:25.681 --> 00:29:27.524
I don't really want to criticize him,

432
00:29:27.641 --> 00:29:32.738
but it was a debacle, really,
for him and for the world.

433
00:29:34.064 --> 00:29:36.817
At that point, I lost a lot of hope

434
00:29:36.942 --> 00:29:40.367
about our ability to prevent the Iraq war.

435
00:29:42.114 --> 00:29:44.617
When you sign your life on the line
for your country

436
00:29:44.741 --> 00:29:46.584
and swear to defend it,

437
00:29:46.702 --> 00:29:48.830
the only thing that you really ask for
in return

438
00:29:48.954 --> 00:29:51.423
is that it be for a good reason,

439
00:29:51.540 --> 00:29:54.965
and I didn't feel it was for a good reason.

440
00:29:57.504 --> 00:29:59.757
Yeah, I was in charge of it,

441
00:29:59.881 --> 00:30:03.135
and when I finished it and thought about it,

442
00:30:03.260 --> 00:30:05.183
I felt miserable because I thought

443
00:30:05.304 --> 00:30:10.401
we had just put a whole array
of circumstantial evidence up

444
00:30:10.517 --> 00:30:14.943
that could be interpreted
in any number of different ways.

445
00:30:15.063 --> 00:30:17.691
And we were probably going to go to war,

446
00:30:17.816 --> 00:30:20.820
and it sort of bothered me.

447
00:30:20.944 --> 00:30:23.242
And now I feel like it was the lowest point,

448
00:30:23.363 --> 00:30:26.833
as I've said before,
in my professional and personal life.

449
00:30:26.950 --> 00:30:28.577
I wish I had resigned.

450
00:30:33.081 --> 00:30:36.051
On this February day,

451
00:30:36.168 --> 00:30:41.174
as this nation stands at the brink of battle,

452
00:30:41.298 --> 00:30:46.850
this chamber is, for the most part,

453
00:30:46.970 --> 00:30:48.643
ominously...

454
00:30:49.806 --> 00:30:51.433
ominously...

455
00:30:52.476 --> 00:30:54.570
dreadfully silent.

456
00:30:56.521 --> 00:30:59.491
You can hear a pin drop. Listen.

457
00:31:03.737 --> 00:31:05.760
You can hear a pin drop.

458
00:31:15.624 --> 00:31:19.754
The battle lines are being
drawn in the Gulf and at home,

459
00:31:19.878 --> 00:31:21.346
these protestors in no doubt

460
00:31:21.463 --> 00:31:24.342
as to whose side they're on
and who they're against.

461
00:31:24.466 --> 00:31:27.345
I feel that I speak for, um,

462
00:31:27.469 --> 00:31:30.814
a growing number of people in this country.

463
00:31:30.931 --> 00:31:33.229
I think if you stopped anyone now on the road

464
00:31:33.350 --> 00:31:35.603
and asked them what they think,
they'd say "No."

465
00:31:35.727 --> 00:31:39.732
I've always been, uh...

466
00:31:39.856 --> 00:31:42.075
quite passionate about CND.

467
00:31:42.192 --> 00:31:44.661
Uh, it kind of sort of goes back

468
00:31:44.778 --> 00:31:48.408
to coming from a family
of conscientious objectors.

469
00:31:48.532 --> 00:31:50.375
So I did what little I could,

470
00:31:50.492 --> 00:31:53.712
in the sense of I could
just talk about it in the media,

471
00:31:53.829 --> 00:31:55.376
express myself.

472
00:31:57.249 --> 00:32:02.130
There was a moment where it felt,
well, we might be able to intervene.

473
00:32:06.591 --> 00:32:10.641
Inspections, not war.
War is not worth dying for.

474
00:32:10.762 --> 00:32:14.938
Inspections, not war. Inspections, not war.

475
00:32:15.058 --> 00:32:17.231
The Iraqis are real people.

476
00:32:17.352 --> 00:32:20.356
The majority of them are under 18 years old.

477
00:32:20.480 --> 00:32:25.202
They're lovely, nice, wonderful people,
and we don't want to see them killed.

478
00:32:26.528 --> 00:32:29.122
February 14th. That would be the day

479
00:32:29.239 --> 00:32:33.494
the Security Council would hear
from the inspectors in Iraq

480
00:32:33.618 --> 00:32:36.337
responsible for the UN inspections.

481
00:32:38.290 --> 00:32:40.133
So important is this event

482
00:32:40.250 --> 00:32:43.754
that all the main foreign ministers
of the United Nations are here,

483
00:32:43.879 --> 00:32:45.973
including America's Colin Powell.

484
00:32:46.089 --> 00:32:47.716
They've insisted on turning up

485
00:32:47.841 --> 00:32:50.139
because this encounter could even determine

486
00:32:50.260 --> 00:32:52.479
the fate of the United Nations.

487
00:32:52.596 --> 00:32:54.598
The chamber of the Security Council
had never been

488
00:32:54.723 --> 00:32:57.317
as full of cameras
as they were in those days.

489
00:33:01.521 --> 00:33:03.615
We walked into this room and looked around.

490
00:33:03.732 --> 00:33:05.905
It was just full of cameras.

491
00:33:08.862 --> 00:33:13.163
How much, if any, is left
of Iraq's weapons of mass destruction

492
00:33:13.283 --> 00:33:16.958
and related prescribed items and programs?

493
00:33:17.078 --> 00:33:20.252
So far, UNMOVIC has not found
any such weapons,

494
00:33:20.373 --> 00:33:23.627
only a small number
of empty chemical munitions.

495
00:33:23.752 --> 00:33:27.222
We have to date found no evidence

496
00:33:27.339 --> 00:33:31.845
of ongoing prohibited nuclear
or nuclear-related activities in Iraq.

497
00:33:31.968 --> 00:33:34.187
As I have just indicated, a number of...

498
00:33:34.304 --> 00:33:36.648
What the inspectors actually said

499
00:33:36.765 --> 00:33:39.109
was very, very unequivocal.

500
00:33:40.101 --> 00:33:42.775
"We have seen no evidence."

501
00:33:42.896 --> 00:33:45.194
They would not, they did not,

502
00:33:45.315 --> 00:33:49.616
provide the US with any excuse to go to war,

503
00:33:49.736 --> 00:33:52.205
and the Security Council knew it.

504
00:34:03.166 --> 00:34:05.715
When the two branches of the march meet here

505
00:34:05.835 --> 00:34:08.054
in Piccadilly Circus tomorrow afternoon,

506
00:34:08.171 --> 00:34:09.923
Eros may well be witnessing

507
00:34:10.048 --> 00:34:13.143
the biggest peacetime demonstration
in British history.

508
00:34:14.594 --> 00:34:18.849
The day before,
the Evening Standard and the Mail

509
00:34:18.974 --> 00:34:22.899
and all the others had a double-page
spread about the march route,

510
00:34:23.019 --> 00:34:25.613
what are the ideal clothes
to wear at a demonstration,

511
00:34:25.730 --> 00:34:28.984
and you suddenly realized
we'd become the mainstream.

512
00:34:29.109 --> 00:34:31.237
Saturday's demonstration
against the war on Iraq

513
00:34:31.361 --> 00:34:34.911
is expected to be the biggest peacetime
rally ever seen in this country.

514
00:34:35.031 --> 00:34:38.752
There will be other protests
from New York to Tokyo.

515
00:34:38.868 --> 00:34:40.461
In the course of the seven weeks,

516
00:34:40.579 --> 00:34:44.209
we must have distributed well
over a million pieces of literature.

517
00:34:44.332 --> 00:34:48.007
All of us became aware that
this thing was gonna be big.

518
00:34:48.128 --> 00:34:51.428
There is a large number of people
that are gonna be on this demonstration

519
00:34:51.548 --> 00:34:53.926
that have never demonstrated before
in their lives.

520
00:34:54.050 --> 00:34:58.180
Middle England are coming out
in their thousands on Saturday,

521
00:34:58.305 --> 00:35:00.774
and that is what is going
to make a difference.

522
00:35:00.890 --> 00:35:03.643
Tonight, final preparations
are underway here in London

523
00:35:03.768 --> 00:35:05.941
for the anti-war rally tomorrow.

524
00:35:06.062 --> 00:35:09.441
It's being billed as the biggest peace
demonstration in recent years.

525
00:35:23.455 --> 00:35:26.709
The rallies of February
15th followed the sun.

526
00:35:26.833 --> 00:35:28.801
They started in the South Pacific.

527
00:35:28.918 --> 00:35:30.841
After that it was New Zealand,

528
00:35:30.962 --> 00:35:33.010
and after that it was Australia,
it was Sydney.

529
00:35:33.131 --> 00:35:37.477
They came from everywhere
and from every walk of life

530
00:35:37.594 --> 00:35:40.143
to walk for life and for peace.

531
00:35:40.263 --> 00:35:43.142
It was an extraordinary display
of people power.

532
00:35:43.266 --> 00:35:46.270
So many voices with one simple message.

533
00:35:46.394 --> 00:35:49.819
No war! No war!

534
00:35:49.939 --> 00:35:53.489
I remember getting on the train
to go into the rally,

535
00:35:53.610 --> 00:35:56.159
and the train was absolutely packed,

536
00:35:56.279 --> 00:35:58.077
and you realized something huge was happening

537
00:35:58.198 --> 00:36:02.078
and it was just a complete cross-section
of liberal concerned Australia.

538
00:36:02.202 --> 00:36:04.671
And then it was North Asia and South Asia.

539
00:36:07.624 --> 00:36:10.093
And then after that it was Malaysia.

540
00:36:10.210 --> 00:36:12.338
It was Indonesia, it was the Philippines,

541
00:36:12.462 --> 00:36:14.314
it was India, it was Pakistan.

542
00:36:18.218 --> 00:36:19.736
And then across Russia.

543
00:36:22.806 --> 00:36:24.934
And then down into Africa.

544
00:36:26.976 --> 00:36:32.198
This is a war about the oil reserves of Iraq.

545
00:36:33.650 --> 00:36:35.027
And then across into Europe.

546
00:36:37.320 --> 00:36:41.166
Roma was full of different marches,

547
00:36:41.282 --> 00:36:46.789
and we found another enormous march
coming against us,

548
00:36:46.913 --> 00:36:48.756
and we don't know who they are.

549
00:36:48.873 --> 00:36:51.626
And no, they are us!

550
00:36:58.299 --> 00:37:02.270
And then there was the huge protest in Spain.

551
00:37:09.936 --> 00:37:13.236
In Barcelona was something
that we couldn't imagine.

552
00:37:13.356 --> 00:37:15.404
It was no march because, in fact,

553
00:37:15.525 --> 00:37:19.200
all passage to the Gràcia
was occupied by people.

554
00:37:19.320 --> 00:37:21.072
It was full of people.

555
00:37:23.783 --> 00:37:27.833
All over Spain it's around five million
people going into the streets.

556
00:37:27.954 --> 00:37:34.007
So it was the biggest demonstration
in the history in the Spanish State.

557
00:37:35.503 --> 00:37:40.509
You're reading all of the wire reports
increasingly from all of these cities,

558
00:37:40.633 --> 00:37:42.601
and you see France, Paris.

559
00:37:46.598 --> 00:37:49.602
Here in France,
everyone is united against the war.

560
00:37:49.726 --> 00:37:53.071
The people, the politicians, the newspapers.

561
00:37:53.188 --> 00:37:57.364
Hundreds of cities.
All of a sudden, this sea of humanity.

562
00:38:09.078 --> 00:38:12.753
And then... we had London.

563
00:38:18.838 --> 00:38:20.385
I thought I was on the wrong march.

564
00:38:20.507 --> 00:38:23.761
I did. I thought this must
be for something else

565
00:38:23.885 --> 00:38:28.015
because there were all these families,
people with pushcarts and babies

566
00:38:28.139 --> 00:38:30.267
and people I'd just never
seen on these things,

567
00:38:30.391 --> 00:38:32.519
and the outpouring of rage from the people.

568
00:38:32.644 --> 00:38:34.897
It was so beautiful.

569
00:38:35.021 --> 00:38:38.946
Really passionate and eloquent and beautiful.

570
00:38:39.067 --> 00:38:41.195
People crying out and shouting.

571
00:38:48.827 --> 00:38:53.674
This proposed war by Britain
is historically unpopular,

572
00:38:53.790 --> 00:38:56.964
and the mother of all focus groups
has descended on London

573
00:38:57.085 --> 00:39:01.306
to bring that fact home to Tony Blair today.

574
00:39:01.422 --> 00:39:04.801
And then the march began,
and we were millions.

575
00:39:04.926 --> 00:39:06.428
It seemed we were millions.

576
00:39:06.553 --> 00:39:10.353
A million people in...
on the streets of London.

577
00:39:10.473 --> 00:39:13.101
There hadn't been a demonstration that size

578
00:39:13.226 --> 00:39:15.228
in anybody in the government,

579
00:39:15.353 --> 00:39:19.699
let alone in Parliament's recollection.

580
00:39:19.816 --> 00:39:24.287
It was... It sort of shut you up.

581
00:39:24.404 --> 00:39:26.907
Virtually everybody I know was on it.

582
00:39:27.031 --> 00:39:29.409
I wish I'd been on it.
I should have been on it.

583
00:39:29.534 --> 00:39:32.959
People like my son and my granddaughters.

584
00:39:33.079 --> 00:39:35.047
He, not being a political activist,

585
00:39:35.164 --> 00:39:37.713
and he was texting me saying
"This is fantastic."

586
00:39:37.834 --> 00:39:39.427
"He can't go to war now."

587
00:39:41.421 --> 00:39:43.594
And it was beautiful.

588
00:39:43.715 --> 00:39:47.185
Many say they've never known
an atmosphere quite like it.

589
00:39:47.302 --> 00:39:48.849
Could that be in part

590
00:39:48.970 --> 00:39:52.565
because so many were moved
to protest for the first time?

591
00:39:52.682 --> 00:39:55.356
I'm not a demonstration
type of person, actually.

592
00:39:55.476 --> 00:39:58.980
I mean, it may well have been the first
demonstration I ever went on.

593
00:39:59.105 --> 00:40:00.607
I think the world's going mad.

594
00:40:00.732 --> 00:40:02.985
I just feel it's got to be stopped somewhere.

595
00:40:03.109 --> 00:40:08.661
Anybody who could come came,
of my lot. And family, I just...

596
00:40:08.781 --> 00:40:10.533
A couple of phone calls, and I should think

597
00:40:10.658 --> 00:40:12.626
we must have been a dozen.

598
00:40:12.744 --> 00:40:15.918
And they loved it and were very proud.

599
00:40:16.039 --> 00:40:19.293
This was the future of humanity,
and people felt it.

600
00:40:19.417 --> 00:40:20.669
That's why they came.

601
00:40:23.963 --> 00:40:25.806
I managed to get friends of mine,

602
00:40:25.924 --> 00:40:30.851
that I never considered for a moment
would come with me, to come that day.

603
00:40:30.970 --> 00:40:35.396
I hadn't been an activist before.
I hadn't protested anything before.

604
00:40:35.516 --> 00:40:38.565
There was a sense of "Come on,
come with me, come and join me."

605
00:40:38.686 --> 00:40:40.108
"We can do this together."

606
00:40:40.229 --> 00:40:42.698
I was very enthusiastic
that this was going to do it.

607
00:40:42.815 --> 00:40:44.567
This was going to stop the Iraq war.

608
00:40:44.692 --> 00:40:48.538
If Blair cared about democracy,
we wouldn't be doing this march

609
00:40:48.655 --> 00:40:50.703
because he would be representing his people.

610
00:40:50.823 --> 00:40:52.871
What Bush and Blair are actually planning

611
00:40:52.992 --> 00:40:55.120
is so disgusting, so bloody lousy.

612
00:40:55.244 --> 00:40:56.461
It's a war for oil.

613
00:40:59.040 --> 00:41:02.385
I don't think it was
until 15th February 2003,

614
00:41:02.502 --> 00:41:06.097
that we really understood
the enormity of the divide

615
00:41:06.214 --> 00:41:08.512
that existed within the country.

616
00:41:10.760 --> 00:41:15.015
And we came to a halt in Whitehall,
and as it happened,

617
00:41:15.139 --> 00:41:20.441
my family and I were dead opposite
the entrance to Downing Street.

618
00:41:20.561 --> 00:41:21.687
And...

619
00:41:22.939 --> 00:41:26.864
a noise went up
that I've never heard before or since.

620
00:41:26.985 --> 00:41:33.083
A kind of visceral, feral grumble,
roar, rising sound.

621
00:41:35.451 --> 00:41:38.625
I remember coming up to Piccadilly Circus,

622
00:41:38.746 --> 00:41:44.753
and there was this just incredible howl
of, I don't know...

623
00:41:44.877 --> 00:41:48.723
Joy, really, as the two demonstrations
came together.

624
00:41:51.551 --> 00:41:55.601
I always feel like this
when, all of a sudden...

625
00:41:57.140 --> 00:41:58.767
millions of ordinary people,

626
00:41:58.891 --> 00:42:02.737
who are told they can't do this,
suddenly do it.

627
00:42:04.022 --> 00:42:07.401
Everybody in the world has a chance today

628
00:42:07.525 --> 00:42:13.282
to say no, absolutely no to war on Iraq.

629
00:42:14.699 --> 00:42:19.205
And there was this real desire.
"Come on, Tony Blair, listen."

630
00:42:19.328 --> 00:42:24.630
"You have to listen.
You can't ignore this many people."

631
00:42:24.751 --> 00:42:26.970
Blair went up to Scotland

632
00:42:27.086 --> 00:42:29.054
to speak at the Scottish
Labour Party Conference.

633
00:42:29.172 --> 00:42:31.971
His speech is greeted with stony silence,

634
00:42:32.091 --> 00:42:33.764
something that never ever happens.

635
00:42:33.885 --> 00:42:38.265
As you watch your TV pictures
of the march, just ponder this.

636
00:42:38.389 --> 00:42:40.733
If there are 500,000 on that march,

637
00:42:40.850 --> 00:42:43.103
that is still less than the number of people

638
00:42:43.227 --> 00:42:46.902
whose deaths Saddam has been responsible for.

639
00:42:47.940 --> 00:42:49.317
If there are one million...

640
00:42:50.943 --> 00:42:53.287
that is still less than the number of people

641
00:42:53.404 --> 00:42:56.658
that died in the wars that he started.

642
00:42:56.783 --> 00:43:03.883
We are starting something really big,
and our first task is peace in Iraq.

643
00:43:03.998 --> 00:43:08.048
Ridding the world of Saddam
would be an act of humanity.

644
00:43:09.212 --> 00:43:12.216
It is leaving him there that is inhumane.

645
00:43:13.299 --> 00:43:18.021
But we must not stop
until we have achieved the objectives

646
00:43:18.137 --> 00:43:21.983
that bring us all to Hyde Park
this afternoon.

647
00:43:24.936 --> 00:43:28.406
We had a counter at the background
of the stage,

648
00:43:28.523 --> 00:43:31.697
and I remember asking
"Can I make that announcement?"

649
00:43:31.818 --> 00:43:33.991
"Can I please make that announcement?"

650
00:43:34.112 --> 00:43:40.290
We had those who thought
we could never, never pull it together,

651
00:43:40.409 --> 00:43:47.167
and we have one and a half million
people marching in London today!

652
00:43:49.836 --> 00:43:51.588
Then, minute by minute,

653
00:43:51.712 --> 00:43:54.807
the phone calls, the feedback,
getting a call from Egypt,

654
00:43:54.924 --> 00:43:57.302
people watching on Egyptian TV

655
00:43:57.426 --> 00:44:00.100
saying, "What is this? What have you done?"

656
00:44:01.139 --> 00:44:04.484
"This is absolutely miraculous.
How have you done this?"

657
00:44:15.111 --> 00:44:19.491
Everyone was in a state of shock
to find, like, one million Brits

658
00:44:19.615 --> 00:44:22.459
who were willing to take to the streets
to protest against the war.

659
00:44:22.577 --> 00:44:24.625
Everyone was shocked that, you know,

660
00:44:24.745 --> 00:44:27.624
there are three million
in the streets of Madrid.

661
00:44:27.748 --> 00:44:29.500
I mean, everybody was shocked.

662
00:44:29.625 --> 00:44:32.003
We were jealous that this
was happening in the West

663
00:44:32.128 --> 00:44:33.880
and not happening in the Arab world.

664
00:44:34.005 --> 00:44:38.385
Millions of Egyptians and Arabs and Muslims

665
00:44:38.509 --> 00:44:40.386
can watch on the TV screens

666
00:44:40.511 --> 00:44:44.641
those white, whisky-drinking infidels
taking to the streets on their behalf,

667
00:44:44.765 --> 00:44:48.736
while in their own countries, you know,
you cannot mobilize on that scale.

668
00:44:48.853 --> 00:44:51.322
The issue was, if they're doing that there,

669
00:44:51.439 --> 00:44:53.692
we should do at least as much here.

670
00:44:53.816 --> 00:44:57.036
In Egypt it was one
of my most depressing days.

671
00:44:57.153 --> 00:45:01.579
We had tiny turnout
for these calls of protest.

672
00:45:06.204 --> 00:45:10.801
On 15th February, we did
actually organize something small.

673
00:45:14.295 --> 00:45:19.517
And, of course,
we had the huge military police

674
00:45:19.634 --> 00:45:21.807
slash military mobilization of the time.

675
00:45:21.928 --> 00:45:24.056
But we knew it's going to happen

676
00:45:24.180 --> 00:45:26.899
because of the international mobilization.

677
00:45:28.976 --> 00:45:30.728
It was very frustrating.

678
00:45:30.853 --> 00:45:35.154
I give so much credit to the activists here

679
00:45:35.274 --> 00:45:37.777
who didn't give up on the Egyptian people,

680
00:45:37.902 --> 00:45:40.496
because I think I might have given up
on the Egyptian people.

681
00:45:42.865 --> 00:45:45.994
That day, 15th February 2003,

682
00:45:46.118 --> 00:45:51.090
gave us a real mobilization power

683
00:45:51.207 --> 00:45:53.551
to get the message across

684
00:45:53.668 --> 00:46:00.426
that whenever the US and the UK
would start their war against Iraq...

685
00:46:01.926 --> 00:46:05.396
we should be on the streets opposing that.

686
00:46:09.475 --> 00:46:12.479
And then, finally, it came to New York.

687
00:46:12.603 --> 00:46:14.605
# This land is your land

688
00:46:17.400 --> 00:46:20.279
# This land is my land

689
00:46:20.403 --> 00:46:22.952
I heard the noise from the outside.

690
00:46:23.072 --> 00:46:27.794
Being out in the street early on
and as the day was going on,

691
00:46:27.910 --> 00:46:30.789
just watching what had to be

692
00:46:30.913 --> 00:46:33.382
tens of thousands, hundreds of thousands.

693
00:46:33.499 --> 00:46:36.127
It just... People kept coming.

694
00:46:37.670 --> 00:46:41.925
# To the Gulf Stream waters

695
00:46:42.967 --> 00:46:44.093
# I tell ya

696
00:46:44.218 --> 00:46:47.267
I needed some milk and some vegetables,

697
00:46:47.388 --> 00:46:52.235
and I went out, and I realized
what the demonstration was about.

698
00:46:52.351 --> 00:46:53.944
One, two, three, four!

699
00:46:54.061 --> 00:46:55.813
# As I went walking

700
00:46:55.938 --> 00:46:57.861
Five, six, seven, eight!

701
00:46:57.982 --> 00:46:59.199
# Down that...

702
00:46:59.317 --> 00:47:03.072
I felt compelled to fly out on a weekend
while I was on active duty

703
00:47:03.195 --> 00:47:07.792
to attend this protest in New York City
on February 15th 2003.

704
00:47:10.411 --> 00:47:14.837
I do remember getting off the subway
and seeing Grand Central Station

705
00:47:14.957 --> 00:47:18.211
and just seeing the people
that were in there,

706
00:47:18.336 --> 00:47:20.179
and it was overwhelming.

707
00:47:20.296 --> 00:47:22.094
I went outside the doors,

708
00:47:22.214 --> 00:47:26.139
and I just remember seeing
just so many people.

709
00:47:26.260 --> 00:47:29.935
Just people for blocks and blocks
and blocks and blocks.

710
00:47:30.056 --> 00:47:33.310
Who knows where they were to?
And you could almost...

711
00:47:33.434 --> 00:47:36.608
If you could look to 125th,
they may have been as far as 125th.

712
00:47:36.729 --> 00:47:41.200
I'm getting chills right now
just talking about it. Uh...

713
00:47:41.317 --> 00:47:43.820
# They tried to stop me

714
00:47:43.944 --> 00:47:48.745
# They put up a sign that said

715
00:47:48.866 --> 00:47:51.665
# Oh, it said "private property"

716
00:47:51.786 --> 00:47:53.880
I felt touched by it.

717
00:47:53.996 --> 00:47:59.924
I felt this is part of
the political drama that is played out.

718
00:48:00.044 --> 00:48:03.969
- Whose street?
- Our street!

719
00:48:04.090 --> 00:48:09.096
I also had some little fear that maybe
someone would identify me

720
00:48:09.220 --> 00:48:15.068
and hoist me up on a truck
as a mascot or something.

721
00:48:15.184 --> 00:48:18.028
But no one did that.

722
00:48:18.145 --> 00:48:20.944
Streets and parks and cities
across the US and the world

723
00:48:21.065 --> 00:48:22.942
were filled with demonstrators today

724
00:48:23.067 --> 00:48:26.446
protesting a possible
US-Ied war against Iraq.

725
00:48:26.570 --> 00:48:29.244
All told, anti-war demonstrations took place

726
00:48:29.365 --> 00:48:32.209
in at least 150 American cities
from coast to coast.

727
00:48:32.326 --> 00:48:37.958
We were attempting something
on this day that was historic.

728
00:48:38.999 --> 00:48:41.127
There was no way to not take notice.

729
00:48:41.252 --> 00:48:44.756
Surely President Bush must be watching,

730
00:48:44.880 --> 00:48:47.884
and surely Tony Blair must be watching.

731
00:48:48.008 --> 00:48:50.431
You know, the size of a protest,
it's like deciding,

732
00:48:50.553 --> 00:48:53.432
well, I'm gonna decide policy
based upon a focus group.

733
00:48:53.556 --> 00:48:55.183
They can't hold us back!

734
00:48:55.307 --> 00:48:58.026
Bush, you can't hold us back!

735
00:48:58.144 --> 00:48:59.987
You can't hold the people!

736
00:49:00.104 --> 00:49:02.152
You can't keep the people down!

737
00:49:02.273 --> 00:49:05.573
# And this land

738
00:49:05.693 --> 00:49:07.695
# Was made for you and me

739
00:49:07.820 --> 00:49:14.294
The people will be heard!
There will not be a war against Iraq!

740
00:49:17.246 --> 00:49:19.340
There was unbelievable sense

741
00:49:19.457 --> 00:49:21.630
that maybe this could make a difference.

742
00:49:21.750 --> 00:49:23.673
Maybe, for the first time in history,

743
00:49:23.794 --> 00:49:27.048
we could actually stop the bad guys, right,

744
00:49:27.173 --> 00:49:29.096
from doing something horrendous.

745
00:49:32.553 --> 00:49:36.683
People are saying no to war!

746
00:49:36.807 --> 00:49:40.027
And we stand in solidarity,

747
00:49:40.144 --> 00:49:44.524
all the people of the world
who are marching at this very moment.

748
00:49:44.648 --> 00:49:48.278
I remember Harry Belafonte and his
long-time great friend Danny Glover,

749
00:49:48.402 --> 00:49:49.995
the great activist actor,

750
00:49:50.112 --> 00:49:54.208
who said "We stand here
on this threshold of history,

751
00:49:54.325 --> 00:49:56.327
and we say to the world..."

752
00:49:56.452 --> 00:50:00.127
Not in our names, not in our names.

753
00:50:00.247 --> 00:50:02.215
"Not in our name."

754
00:50:02.333 --> 00:50:06.509
And I remember the crowd picked it up,
and it was echoing.

755
00:50:12.259 --> 00:50:15.012
And I remember thinking,
I will never again...

756
00:50:16.180 --> 00:50:18.103
have an opportunity like this,

757
00:50:18.224 --> 00:50:22.445
to be present at a moment
that is changing the world.

758
00:50:22.561 --> 00:50:25.064
And all a sudden I felt elated,

759
00:50:25.189 --> 00:50:27.738
and I felt, "Of course we can stop the war,

760
00:50:27.858 --> 00:50:29.781
because we are so many people,

761
00:50:29.902 --> 00:50:32.781
and there's no leader
in any country in the world

762
00:50:32.905 --> 00:50:39.003
that could go ahead with a war when
they see a global movement saying no."

763
00:50:43.541 --> 00:50:45.384
- What do we want?
- Peace!

764
00:50:45.501 --> 00:50:47.754
- When do we want it?
- Now!

765
00:50:47.878 --> 00:50:50.631
Let you remember
and let your children remember

766
00:50:50.756 --> 00:50:57.355
that on February 15th you were here,
joined by 35 million people.

767
00:50:57.471 --> 00:50:59.815
Stop the war!

768
00:51:00.808 --> 00:51:03.311
Stop the war!

769
00:51:03.435 --> 00:51:04.937
Stop the war!

770
00:51:05.062 --> 00:51:08.783
We had the first global demonstration.

771
00:51:10.901 --> 00:51:14.451
There were demonstrations
in what, every continent,

772
00:51:14.572 --> 00:51:18.668
and 600 cities around the world
had demonstrations,

773
00:51:18.784 --> 00:51:21.207
including a demonstration in Antarctica.

774
00:51:35.384 --> 00:51:38.012
Saying this thing even happened
in Antarctica is like saying

775
00:51:38.137 --> 00:51:39.980
it happened on the moon
or it happened on Mars.

776
00:51:41.223 --> 00:51:42.896
I think Antarctica stills exists

777
00:51:43.017 --> 00:51:45.736
as this sort of mythological place
to most people,

778
00:51:45.853 --> 00:51:47.901
that it is the end of the earth.

779
00:51:48.022 --> 00:51:52.027
And it's remarkable to people
that there are people there at all.

780
00:51:57.656 --> 00:52:00.205
We knew that there was demonstrations

781
00:52:00.326 --> 00:52:02.374
gonna be going on around the world
for February 15th,

782
00:52:02.494 --> 00:52:04.121
and we knew it was gonna be really big.

783
00:52:04.246 --> 00:52:07.967
And I had a subscription to The Nation
that was coming to me in McMurdo,

784
00:52:08.083 --> 00:52:10.836
and I was reading a column
by Alexander Coburn,

785
00:52:10.961 --> 00:52:13.805
and he was talking about the upcoming
February 15th demonstration.

786
00:52:13.922 --> 00:52:16.641
He was saying, "Even in Antarctica
there's gonna be demonstrations."

787
00:52:16.759 --> 00:52:19.137
I thought, "Huh? Who would that be?"

788
00:52:19.261 --> 00:52:21.138
And I thought, "Wait a minute, he means us."

789
00:52:21.263 --> 00:52:24.813
And so, I thought, "Well, we can't let
down the great Alexander Coburn,

790
00:52:24.933 --> 00:52:26.355
so we're gonna have to do something."

791
00:52:26.477 --> 00:52:29.731
And so we got this one together
in fairly short order.

792
00:52:31.523 --> 00:52:35.448
Saying what you think isn't something
that's easily done down there.

793
00:52:35.569 --> 00:52:39.574
First we were told no protest
at all would be tolerated,

794
00:52:39.698 --> 00:52:40.790
and I think they were saying,

795
00:52:40.908 --> 00:52:45.709
"Well, it's OK to be for peace,
but you can't be against the war."

796
00:52:45.829 --> 00:52:50.175
"You do that, you'll go home in a box."

797
00:52:50.292 --> 00:52:52.545
"Try that, you'll go home in a box."

798
00:52:52.670 --> 00:52:53.717
I'm not sure I was aware

799
00:52:53.837 --> 00:52:56.431
that you could actually lose your job
for stuff like this

800
00:52:56.548 --> 00:52:58.642
until I actually lost my job
for stuff like this.

801
00:52:58.759 --> 00:53:01.262
And, that said, even if I'd known,

802
00:53:01.387 --> 00:53:03.310
I think I would have done
just the same thing.

803
00:53:06.642 --> 00:53:10.146
If we could get enough people
who were willing to do it,

804
00:53:10.270 --> 00:53:14.650
that just was hopeful to me,

805
00:53:14.775 --> 00:53:16.994
and when we did, it was joyful to me.

806
00:53:20.531 --> 00:53:24.206
And so we fervently hoped
that we could be part

807
00:53:24.326 --> 00:53:27.500
of this movement that was going on,
this February 15th movement.

808
00:53:27.621 --> 00:53:30.625
Just... we want this to stop.
We don't want this to happen.

809
00:53:36.463 --> 00:53:38.136
- This is how we'd look.
- That's right.

810
00:53:38.257 --> 00:53:40.555
And there was 70 of us
who looked just like this.

811
00:53:49.226 --> 00:53:52.651
This is "Democracy Now."

812
00:53:52.771 --> 00:53:55.741
The world says no to war.

813
00:53:55.858 --> 00:53:59.533
Sites included Australia, Johannesburg,
Tel Aviv, Syria,

814
00:53:59.653 --> 00:54:02.827
Tokyo, Bangladesh, South Korea,
Hong Kong, Thailand,

815
00:54:02.948 --> 00:54:05.292
Puerto Rico, Brazil, East Timor, India,

816
00:54:05.409 --> 00:54:08.083
and even the South Pole.

817
00:54:40.319 --> 00:54:43.664
And when, the next day, The New York Times,

818
00:54:43.781 --> 00:54:47.502
on the front page above the fold,
said this great truth,

819
00:54:47.618 --> 00:54:50.747
"Once again there are two superpowers
in the world."

820
00:54:50.871 --> 00:54:54.125
"The United States
and global public opinion."

821
00:54:54.249 --> 00:54:55.592
That was huge.

822
00:54:58.045 --> 00:55:00.298
Some of my friends in the anti-war movement

823
00:55:00.422 --> 00:55:02.390
got rather puffed up about it.

824
00:55:02.508 --> 00:55:05.682
I thought they were gonna do t-shirts.
"Second Superpower."

825
00:55:05.803 --> 00:55:07.976
You know, there was a bit of that.

826
00:55:08.096 --> 00:55:11.441
My view was, this is fantastic.
We worked for it.

827
00:55:11.558 --> 00:55:14.732
It's not gonna last for long,
but it's a marker.

828
00:55:14.853 --> 00:55:18.232
It was a big marker. Tremendous.

829
00:55:18.357 --> 00:55:22.533
I think I made an observation
at a critical period

830
00:55:22.653 --> 00:55:27.750
as our country was spooling up for war,
that there was another power out there,

831
00:55:27.866 --> 00:55:31.245
and I think we live at our peril
in ignoring it

832
00:55:31.370 --> 00:55:33.338
and certainly in denigrating it,

833
00:55:33.455 --> 00:55:36.379
because that tends to be the next generation,

834
00:55:36.500 --> 00:55:38.127
and that is the future.

835
00:55:51.640 --> 00:55:56.020
I was actually overseas
when the big demonstration took place.

836
00:55:57.437 --> 00:55:59.565
The whole of my family went on it.

837
00:56:01.024 --> 00:56:03.277
My parents, my sisters.

838
00:56:03.402 --> 00:56:06.201
There was a real feeling that actually
you could make a difference

839
00:56:06.321 --> 00:56:09.791
because going to war was an absolute nonsense

840
00:56:09.908 --> 00:56:12.752
and there had to be another way out of this.

841
00:56:12.870 --> 00:56:15.214
I was trying my best to see

842
00:56:15.330 --> 00:56:17.424
whether there was anything
behind the scenes I could do

843
00:56:17.541 --> 00:56:22.547
to, you know, hopefully avert the war.

844
00:56:25.591 --> 00:56:28.470
To an extent, Saddam Hussein
was now a cornered animal,

845
00:56:28.594 --> 00:56:33.191
and an animal when they're cornered
is extremely dangerous.

846
00:56:33.307 --> 00:56:35.401
With the build-up to the war,

847
00:56:35.517 --> 00:56:40.614
the likelihood was that he was going
to end up being executed or killed.

848
00:56:40.731 --> 00:56:44.076
And therefore I felt that
there was a reasonable chance

849
00:56:44.192 --> 00:56:45.660
in trying to persuade him

850
00:56:45.777 --> 00:56:48.326
that he should bow out
and go to another country.

851
00:56:48.447 --> 00:56:51.326
We'd definitely had these conversations
beforehand,

852
00:56:51.450 --> 00:56:53.794
and there definitely did
seem to be an alternative

853
00:56:53.911 --> 00:56:55.288
than doing what we did.

854
00:56:55.412 --> 00:56:57.665
I was fortunate enough
to know Nelson Mandela.

855
00:56:57.789 --> 00:57:00.338
One power,

856
00:57:00.459 --> 00:57:07.593
with a President who has no foresight,
who cannot think properly...

857
00:57:08.751 --> 00:57:15.650
...is now wanting to plunge the world
into a holocaust,

858
00:57:15.766 --> 00:57:21.193
and I'm happy that the people
of the world are standing up.

859
00:57:21.313 --> 00:57:24.362
Nelson Mandela had spoken vehemently

860
00:57:24.483 --> 00:57:28.078
out against the idea of the war
and against the invasion,

861
00:57:28.195 --> 00:57:34.043
and so I felt that there was a chance
that if he came to see Saddam Hussein,

862
00:57:34.159 --> 00:57:40.007
and if he agreed to fly out
on the same plane as Saddam Hussein,

863
00:57:40.123 --> 00:57:41.921
that we might have a success.

864
00:57:43.961 --> 00:57:46.680
Kofi Annan also agreed to go on the trip,

865
00:57:46.797 --> 00:57:50.097
so we arranged a plane to go to South Africa

866
00:57:50.217 --> 00:57:53.391
to pick them up to take them to Baghdad.

867
00:58:05.983 --> 00:58:10.159
I can think of nothing that
Saddam Hussein could do diplomatically.

868
00:58:10.278 --> 00:58:12.701
I think that time is now over.

869
00:58:14.241 --> 00:58:16.960
The time for diplomacy has passed.

870
00:58:17.077 --> 00:58:21.048
Sadly, the very week that they were due

871
00:58:21.164 --> 00:58:26.887
to go to Baghdad to sit down
with Saddam Hussein,

872
00:58:27.004 --> 00:58:29.757
bombing started,
and the visit never took place.

873
00:58:32.926 --> 00:58:38.103
Now, whether the Americans or British

874
00:58:38.223 --> 00:58:40.897
had discovered what was going on,
we don't know.

875
00:58:41.018 --> 00:58:45.740
I mean, we had on purpose
not informed the authorities

876
00:58:45.856 --> 00:58:48.905
because we felt they might
bring the war forward

877
00:58:49.026 --> 00:58:52.326
if they knew this trip was to take place.

878
00:58:52.446 --> 00:58:56.371
But I still think every time
he looks at the news,

879
00:58:56.491 --> 00:58:59.665
he must think, "If only, you know,
if only it had worked."

880
00:58:59.786 --> 00:59:03.165
It was that close to having a solution.

881
00:59:13.133 --> 00:59:15.306
After the big march, February 15th,

882
00:59:15.427 --> 00:59:18.397
it was clear that the waving of placards
wasn't going to be any good

883
00:59:18.513 --> 00:59:20.356
and writing letters
wasn't going to be any good.

884
00:59:24.061 --> 00:59:26.814
And I decided, you know,
I was going to do something,

885
00:59:26.938 --> 00:59:29.657
and I was prepared to get arrested,
and da-dee-da-dee-da.

886
00:59:32.611 --> 00:59:35.114
So my friend Dave and I
were talking about it one evening.

887
00:59:35.238 --> 00:59:39.209
"Where would the most effective
place to paint a slogan in Sydney be?"

888
00:59:39.326 --> 00:59:41.545
And I said, "Well, to put it bluntly,

889
00:59:41.661 --> 00:59:43.880
it's on the sails of the Sydney Opera House."

890
00:59:44.915 --> 00:59:46.542
And it was this sort of real sinking feeling

891
00:59:46.666 --> 00:59:48.418
because, of course, he was absolutely right.

892
00:59:48.543 --> 00:59:52.764
It was the, you know, the iconic place
that the thing,

893
00:59:52.881 --> 00:59:56.351
that the message was inescapable,
and it was, you know...

894
00:59:56.468 --> 00:59:58.311
I sort of swallowed three times
and said, "OK."

895
00:59:58.428 --> 01:00:00.772
There I am, feeling very strongly about this.

896
01:00:00.889 --> 01:00:03.233
Do I feel strongly enough to, you know,

897
01:00:03.350 --> 01:00:05.569
probably to go to prison
for quite a long time?

898
01:00:05.685 --> 01:00:08.905
He said, "I could well get deported
if we do this,"

899
01:00:09.022 --> 01:00:12.242
and I said, "Well, ring me back
when you've thought that one through."

900
01:00:12.359 --> 01:00:15.989
And sure enough, he rang me back.

901
01:00:16.113 --> 01:00:19.208
Well, yes, I mean, the answer was yes.

902
01:00:19.324 --> 01:00:23.875
It was this once-in-a-Iifetime thing
where if we didn't...

903
01:00:23.995 --> 01:00:27.249
Even if we failed to stop it,
at least if the world could see

904
01:00:27.374 --> 01:00:30.628
that the people didn't want
what was being done in their name.

905
01:00:34.548 --> 01:00:37.017
With death-defying bravado,

906
01:00:37.134 --> 01:00:40.764
the anti-war movement's protest
scaled new heights.

907
01:00:42.889 --> 01:00:44.516
It was all very amateurish.

908
01:00:44.641 --> 01:00:46.393
We had paint, we had backpacks.

909
01:00:46.518 --> 01:00:49.112
We had great long paint rollers,

910
01:00:49.229 --> 01:00:51.823
like three or four of them to join together.

911
01:00:51.940 --> 01:00:56.195
We climbed up without any trouble.
Dave climbed up after me.

912
01:00:56.319 --> 01:01:00.540
Will, being a nonsmoker,
got to the top way quicker than I did.

913
01:01:00.657 --> 01:01:02.409
When he got up there, he said,

914
01:01:02.534 --> 01:01:05.208
"Will, I should tell you
I'm very scared of heights."

915
01:01:05.328 --> 01:01:07.797
I felt sick.

916
01:01:12.294 --> 01:01:14.922
This sort of overwhelming feeling
of not wanting to stuff it up.

917
01:01:15.046 --> 01:01:17.549
This sort of terror that you were
going to paint "No Wa"

918
01:01:17.674 --> 01:01:19.642
or that the "N" would be the wrong way around

919
01:01:19.759 --> 01:01:21.227
or, you know, something like that.

920
01:01:23.513 --> 01:01:26.858
To my amazement,
the font came out beautifully.

921
01:01:30.145 --> 01:01:31.647
And the police finally arrived

922
01:01:31.771 --> 01:01:34.399
just as we were touching it up
for the last time,

923
01:01:34.524 --> 01:01:38.404
and I said to the policeman,
"Can I just finish this bit?"

924
01:01:38.528 --> 01:01:45.127
And he was very polite. He said,
"No, I think you've done enough."

925
01:01:45.243 --> 01:01:48.292
This afternoon, the two men
were released on bail.

926
01:01:48.413 --> 01:01:51.257
We're charged with malicious damage,
which is quite ironic,

927
01:01:51.374 --> 01:01:53.593
because if war isn't the ultimate
malicious damage,

928
01:01:53.710 --> 01:01:55.178
I don't know what is.

929
01:01:55.295 --> 01:01:58.014
But freedom for Will Saunders
was short-lived.

930
01:01:58.131 --> 01:01:59.724
A scientist from Britain,

931
01:01:59.841 --> 01:02:03.891
he was quickly rearrested
by immigration officers.

932
01:02:31.539 --> 01:02:33.712
People say this place doesn't matter anymore,

933
01:02:33.833 --> 01:02:38.179
but hours before a war,
your MP can vote for or against it.

934
01:02:38.296 --> 01:02:42.176
And if that doesn't matter,
I simply don't know what does.

935
01:02:42.300 --> 01:02:47.272
The more momentous the particular
decision you're dealing with,

936
01:02:47.389 --> 01:02:50.518
the more you feel that obligation to do it

937
01:02:50.642 --> 01:02:52.736
on the basis of your own analysis
of what's best.

938
01:02:52.852 --> 01:02:53.978
And...

939
01:02:54.104 --> 01:02:56.573
But isn't it better to carry
the country with you?

940
01:02:56.690 --> 01:03:00.820
Of course. Of course it would be better
to carry the country with you,

941
01:03:00.944 --> 01:03:03.413
but the demonstration indicated

942
01:03:03.530 --> 01:03:06.909
that a great seam of the country
wasn't with us.

943
01:03:07.033 --> 01:03:08.706
What do you do then?

944
01:03:09.661 --> 01:03:13.291
You look to Parliament for support,

945
01:03:13.415 --> 01:03:16.259
and if you can't get support
in Parliament, then you don't do it.

946
01:03:17.836 --> 01:03:20.430
Now, the night before, Robin Cook resigned

947
01:03:20.547 --> 01:03:24.097
and made an astounding resignation speech.

948
01:03:24.217 --> 01:03:28.222
Iraq probably has no weapons
of mass destruction

949
01:03:28.346 --> 01:03:31.350
in the commonly understood sense of the term.

950
01:03:31.474 --> 01:03:33.226
Namely, a credible device

951
01:03:33.351 --> 01:03:37.572
capable of being delivered
against a strategic city target.

952
01:03:37.689 --> 01:03:41.319
It probably does still have biological toxins

953
01:03:41.443 --> 01:03:44.287
and battlefield chemical munitions.

954
01:03:44.404 --> 01:03:46.953
But it's had them since the 1980s,

955
01:03:47.073 --> 01:03:50.794
when US companies sold Saddam anthrax agents

956
01:03:50.910 --> 01:03:55.666
and the then-British government approved
chemical and munitions factories.

957
01:03:55.790 --> 01:04:01.342
Why is it now so urgent
that we should take military action

958
01:04:01.463 --> 01:04:05.809
to disarm a military capacity
that has been there for 20 years

959
01:04:05.925 --> 01:04:07.927
and which we helped to create?

960
01:04:08.053 --> 01:04:09.225
Hear, hear.

961
01:04:09.346 --> 01:04:13.476
Mr. Speaker,
the longer I have served in this place,

962
01:04:13.600 --> 01:04:15.602
the greater the respect I have

963
01:04:15.727 --> 01:04:18.947
for the good sense and the collective
wisdom of the British people.

964
01:04:19.064 --> 01:04:20.281
Hear, hear.

965
01:04:20.398 --> 01:04:26.906
I intend to join those tomorrow night
who vote against military action now.

966
01:04:27.030 --> 01:04:30.000
It is for that reason and that reason alone,

967
01:04:30.116 --> 01:04:33.461
and with a heavy heart,
that I resign from the government.

968
01:04:33.578 --> 01:04:35.876
Hear, hear!

969
01:04:37.248 --> 01:04:39.421
It made a most profound impact.

970
01:04:39.542 --> 01:04:41.169
I think for the first time in my life,

971
01:04:41.294 --> 01:04:45.299
I heard people actually clapping in
the House of Commons when he sat down.

972
01:04:45.423 --> 01:04:48.848
Because normally you don't do that.
You just say "Hear, hear."

973
01:04:48.968 --> 01:04:52.188
But people actually applauded him
as he sat down.

974
01:04:56.810 --> 01:04:58.437
And then, all through the day,

975
01:04:58.561 --> 01:05:02.407
MPs that were thought to be
skeptical about the war

976
01:05:02.524 --> 01:05:07.655
were being hauled in to see
Tony Blair or Gordon Brown,

977
01:05:07.779 --> 01:05:10.658
and Blair was sort of straight at them
saying, "OK,

978
01:05:10.782 --> 01:05:13.080
are you with me or against me?"

979
01:05:13.201 --> 01:05:15.750
And there were all kinds of deals done,
no doubt.

980
01:05:15.870 --> 01:05:17.497
The Noes to the left 149...

981
01:05:17.622 --> 01:05:19.670
We had estimated at the start of the day

982
01:05:19.791 --> 01:05:21.793
there were possibly 200 Labour MPs,

983
01:05:21.918 --> 01:05:24.637
more than half the Parliamentary
Labour Party, a crucial figure,

984
01:05:24.754 --> 01:05:26.677
who were opposed to the war.

985
01:05:26.798 --> 01:05:29.517
By the end of the day,
that had come down to 139.

986
01:05:29.634 --> 01:05:35.482
The Ayes to the right 412,
the Noes to the left 149.

987
01:05:35.598 --> 01:05:37.225
The Ayes have it.

988
01:05:42.605 --> 01:05:45.154
An awful day because of the consequences.

989
01:05:45.275 --> 01:05:49.826
MPs knowingly voted for lies to go to war,

990
01:05:49.946 --> 01:05:52.540
which has killed thousands of people.

991
01:05:53.783 --> 01:05:58.163
Back! Get back! Get back! Move!

992
01:06:00.248 --> 01:06:03.092
Many of them now come out and say,

993
01:06:03.209 --> 01:06:09.433
"If we knew then what we know now,
we wouldn't have supported the war."

994
01:06:09.549 --> 01:06:13.474
"We wouldn't have believed Tony Blair.
We were misled."

995
01:06:13.595 --> 01:06:15.313
It's rubbish.

996
01:06:15.430 --> 01:06:19.776
When you had two million people
telling you the truth

997
01:06:19.893 --> 01:06:23.443
and giving you the strong case
for why this war would be a disaster,

998
01:06:23.563 --> 01:06:27.113
you cannot say you did not know.
You just did not care.

999
01:06:27.233 --> 01:06:29.201
This was when people suddenly realized

1000
01:06:29.319 --> 01:06:32.448
well, what is a democracy
if you can demonstrate like this

1001
01:06:32.572 --> 01:06:35.325
but it doesn't make any difference
to what happens?

1002
01:06:35.450 --> 01:06:41.048
And war suddenly happened
one night in March 2003.

1003
01:06:53.092 --> 01:06:56.187
At this hour, American and coalition forces

1004
01:06:56.304 --> 01:07:00.605
are in the early stages of
military operations to disarm Iraq,

1005
01:07:00.725 --> 01:07:03.274
to free its people, and to defend the world.

1006
01:07:03.394 --> 01:07:07.695
Tonight British servicemen and women
are engaged from air, land and sea.

1007
01:07:07.815 --> 01:07:11.115
Their mission, to remove Saddam Hussein
from power

1008
01:07:11.236 --> 01:07:13.659
and disarm Iraq of its weapons.

1009
01:07:13.780 --> 01:07:15.782
On my orders, coalition forces have begun

1010
01:07:15.907 --> 01:07:19.036
striking selected targets
of military importance

1011
01:07:19.160 --> 01:07:21.834
to undermine Saddam Hussein's
ability to wage war.

1012
01:07:21.955 --> 01:07:24.253
I hope the Iraqi people hear this message.

1013
01:07:24.374 --> 01:07:25.921
We are with you.

1014
01:07:26.042 --> 01:07:29.387
Our enemy is not you
but your barbarous rulers.

1015
01:07:35.343 --> 01:07:37.266
And then "shock and awe" came.

1016
01:07:39.138 --> 01:07:42.642
And then, and then the horror.

1017
01:07:45.645 --> 01:07:47.318
It was over.

1018
01:08:09.961 --> 01:08:12.214
I think a lot of us cried.

1019
01:08:13.256 --> 01:08:17.682
And a lot of us screamed in fury

1020
01:08:17.802 --> 01:08:20.601
that these demons had started this war.

1021
01:08:27.895 --> 01:08:32.901
When I'm watching these missiles or rockets

1022
01:08:33.026 --> 01:08:36.246
or whatever they were flying across Baghdad,

1023
01:08:36.362 --> 01:08:40.583
it's kind of like when you see imagery
of the World Trade Center.

1024
01:08:40.700 --> 01:08:46.548
To most people, it's this iconic image
of a tower burning.

1025
01:08:46.664 --> 01:08:50.089
To me, it's an image of my brother dying

1026
01:08:50.209 --> 01:08:54.259
because it's very, very real
because I know he's on the 106th floor.

1027
01:08:59.469 --> 01:09:02.769
There's a piece in all of us
that intuitively knows

1028
01:09:02.889 --> 01:09:07.190
that someone's being killed
and some family is being harmed.

1029
01:09:07.310 --> 01:09:10.689
I think kind of the challenge for all of us

1030
01:09:10.813 --> 01:09:15.284
is how much of that are we willing to let in

1031
01:09:15.401 --> 01:09:19.781
and how much is too much to bear
and we have to turn it off.

1032
01:09:32.502 --> 01:09:36.006
When the hope was dashed and the war began,

1033
01:09:36.130 --> 01:09:38.849
I think that depressed people,

1034
01:09:38.966 --> 01:09:42.470
and it led to a simplistic concept

1035
01:09:42.595 --> 01:09:45.815
that we had the biggest demonstration
in history

1036
01:09:45.932 --> 01:09:47.980
and nothing happened, so we're giving up.

1037
01:09:50.144 --> 01:09:52.146
The morale just dropped.

1038
01:09:57.652 --> 01:10:00.246
It's hard to put "George Bush"
and "brilliant" in the same sentence,

1039
01:10:00.363 --> 01:10:02.491
but one of his more brilliant moves

1040
01:10:02.615 --> 01:10:04.788
was just to see 20 million people
around the world

1041
01:10:04.909 --> 01:10:07.037
saying, "We want you to do something,"

1042
01:10:07.161 --> 01:10:10.506
and then him turn around and say,
"We're gonna do it anyway

1043
01:10:10.623 --> 01:10:12.546
and you have no power."

1044
01:10:12.709 --> 01:10:14.962
All that sense of hope and possibility

1045
01:10:15.086 --> 01:10:18.386
that maybe we could prevent this,
we couldn't in the end.

1046
01:10:18.506 --> 01:10:20.133
We were still not strong enough.

1047
01:10:21.843 --> 01:10:24.813
Everybody please onto the sidewalks
in an orderly fashion.

1048
01:10:24.929 --> 01:10:27.432
Everybody on... Keep on walking.

1049
01:10:27.557 --> 01:10:32.529
Once you get past a demonstration,

1050
01:10:32.645 --> 01:10:35.068
a big demonstration
or a couple of big demonstrations,

1051
01:10:35.189 --> 01:10:36.611
what do you do?

1052
01:10:41.738 --> 01:10:44.742
We can identify probably
in retrospect positive things,

1053
01:10:44.866 --> 01:10:48.621
and maybe we averted other conflicts,
and all that kind of stuff,

1054
01:10:48.745 --> 01:10:50.668
but we didn't stop the Iraq War.

1055
01:10:50.788 --> 01:10:54.167
That was something that I think,
you know, it was painful.

1056
01:10:54.292 --> 01:10:57.262
And it made a lot of us then reflect
on why have we failed,

1057
01:10:57.378 --> 01:11:02.430
and certainly for me, now running 38 Degrees,

1058
01:11:02.550 --> 01:11:06.600
I think I can see the roots
of some of my thinking and my ideas

1059
01:11:06.721 --> 01:11:10.396
which lead me to want to start
a people-powered campaigning movement.

1060
01:11:10.516 --> 01:11:14.237
That did come out of the experience,
I think, of feeling the failure.

1061
01:11:14.353 --> 01:11:16.401
I got the sense from the people on the podium

1062
01:11:16.522 --> 01:11:18.900
that they in a way felt the job was done,

1063
01:11:19.025 --> 01:11:21.778
that this was gonna change
the government's opinion.

1064
01:11:21.903 --> 01:11:25.908
Now, we will never know
what Tony Blair and his advisers

1065
01:11:26.032 --> 01:11:30.378
were thinking on the eve of that day.

1066
01:11:33.664 --> 01:11:37.009
I'm sure they gambled on us not coming back.

1067
01:11:37.126 --> 01:11:42.098
Now, I really believe that
if we'd have come back the next weekend,

1068
01:11:42.215 --> 01:11:43.717
they may not have changed then,

1069
01:11:43.841 --> 01:11:45.639
but they would have certainly been nervous.

1070
01:11:45.760 --> 01:11:47.979
But if we'd have come back
the weekend after that,

1071
01:11:48.095 --> 01:11:50.644
then you never know what would have happened.

1072
01:11:50.765 --> 01:11:54.144
And in that sense,

1073
01:11:54.268 --> 01:11:59.195
I feel very frustrated
and a deep sense of regret

1074
01:11:59.315 --> 01:12:03.491
about the fact
that we just didn't finish the job.

1075
01:12:03.611 --> 01:12:08.367
I did believe, I think as many of us
believed on that march,

1076
01:12:08.491 --> 01:12:11.290
that we could actually change something,

1077
01:12:11.410 --> 01:12:15.415
and the fact that we couldn't
has stuck in our craws ever since.

1078
01:12:15.540 --> 01:12:18.089
It was a huge missed opportunity

1079
01:12:18.209 --> 01:12:22.305
because I don't think the march
in itself would ever stop the war

1080
01:12:22.421 --> 01:12:27.143
because people go home
and the government can live with that.

1081
01:12:27.260 --> 01:12:31.310
What they can't live with
is serious organization,

1082
01:12:31.430 --> 01:12:34.104
and that's what we needed out of that.

1083
01:12:34.225 --> 01:12:38.150
You have a couple of days
of mass demonstrations

1084
01:12:38.271 --> 01:12:41.150
during a weekend in London.

1085
01:12:41.274 --> 01:12:43.242
What does that do to the powers that be?

1086
01:12:43.359 --> 01:12:45.327
Nothing, nothing.

1087
01:12:45.444 --> 01:12:47.947
You need to escalate.

1088
01:12:55.705 --> 01:13:00.836
We had this decision
that on the day of the attack,

1089
01:13:00.960 --> 01:13:04.885
on 1:00pm, we're going to assemble
in Tahrir Square.

1090
01:13:05.006 --> 01:13:08.476
Once the strikes begin, we all go to Tahrir.

1091
01:13:08.593 --> 01:13:12.314
Once the war starts, at 12 noon,

1092
01:13:12.430 --> 01:13:15.934
everybody would go out
on the streets towards Tahrir.

1093
01:13:16.058 --> 01:13:18.106
So I went with my high heels,

1094
01:13:18.227 --> 01:13:21.481
thinking it was going to be
just an hour-Iong demonstration

1095
01:13:21.606 --> 01:13:24.325
and that the anti-riot police
are just going to break it,

1096
01:13:24.442 --> 01:13:31.417
and, you know, I had evening plans,
and, uh, I went home at midnight.

1097
01:13:35.202 --> 01:13:37.330
That's when hell broke loose in Egypt.

1098
01:13:39.749 --> 01:13:42.969
You had the biggest protests

1099
01:13:43.085 --> 01:13:47.511
that this capital had witnessed since 1977.

1100
01:13:47.632 --> 01:13:49.384
Young people, old people.

1101
01:13:49.508 --> 01:13:51.602
Poor people, rich people,
middle class people.

1102
01:13:51.719 --> 01:13:53.562
Men, women, students.

1103
01:13:53.679 --> 01:13:55.352
Everybody was there. Everybody was there.

1104
01:13:57.516 --> 01:14:02.272
I arrived on Tahrir Square with 15 people.

1105
01:14:04.190 --> 01:14:07.444
14 out of the 15
never demonstrated in their lives

1106
01:14:07.568 --> 01:14:10.868
before 20th March 2003.

1107
01:14:10.988 --> 01:14:15.368
It was like, for the first time,
we could see a popular movement.

1108
01:14:17.286 --> 01:14:21.587
From 30,000 to 40,000
or 50,000, even, protestors

1109
01:14:21.707 --> 01:14:24.460
were in run-in clashes in with the police.

1110
01:14:25.920 --> 01:14:27.968
Briefly taking over Tahrir.

1111
01:14:28.089 --> 01:14:30.183
That really was a turning point.

1112
01:14:30.299 --> 01:14:33.974
The protests here were huge

1113
01:14:34.095 --> 01:14:36.518
on the night of the Iraq invasion.

1114
01:14:39.433 --> 01:14:41.936
It was the first time I had ever seen

1115
01:14:42.061 --> 01:14:46.658
that protestors had overwhelmed
the security forces.

1116
01:14:50.444 --> 01:14:52.867
They withdrew,
and I remember a comrade, a friend,

1117
01:14:52.989 --> 01:14:55.742
said, "Oh, my God, oh, my God, oh, my God."

1118
01:14:55.866 --> 01:14:59.416
For the first time, the really first time,

1119
01:14:59.537 --> 01:15:05.544
we are able to win this small victory.

1120
01:15:06.877 --> 01:15:08.971
The American occupation of Iraq

1121
01:15:09.088 --> 01:15:13.059
is the occupation of the entire Arab world,

1122
01:15:13.175 --> 01:15:15.052
including Egypt,

1123
01:15:15.177 --> 01:15:19.603
and therefore, the impotency
of our regime is very clear.

1124
01:15:26.355 --> 01:15:31.236
That's exactly when I was thinking,
and others,

1125
01:15:31.360 --> 01:15:36.742
that if we were triple that number,
or four times that number,

1126
01:15:36.866 --> 01:15:38.914
we could take down Mubarak.

1127
01:15:40.077 --> 01:15:41.875
Little did we know

1128
01:15:41.996 --> 01:15:46.877
that that was a rehearsal
for the 2011 revolution.

1129
01:15:49.712 --> 01:15:52.591
That spring of 2003

1130
01:15:52.715 --> 01:15:55.559
was really the beginning
of the democracy movement.

1131
01:16:03.142 --> 01:16:05.645
Iraq was torn to shreds.

1132
01:16:05.770 --> 01:16:09.741
It was a consequence of the invasion,
but it wasn't presented that way.

1133
01:16:12.109 --> 01:16:16.956
Like the Fallujah attack,
which was a major war crime.

1134
01:16:18.407 --> 01:16:22.002
For example, I haven't seen a word
in the United States,

1135
01:16:22.119 --> 01:16:23.712
in the mainstream, at least,

1136
01:16:23.829 --> 01:16:26.799
about the fact that the radiation levels

1137
01:16:26.916 --> 01:16:30.796
and effects of excessive radiation
in Fallujah

1138
01:16:30.920 --> 01:16:32.843
are apparently worse than Hiroshima.

1139
01:16:42.306 --> 01:16:47.688
We know exactly how many people were
killed on September 11th, exactly.

1140
01:16:47.812 --> 01:16:51.316
We have no idea how many civilians
were killed in Iraq.

1141
01:16:51.440 --> 01:16:52.657
Why is that?

1142
01:16:54.735 --> 01:16:57.659
Hey, back up! Back up!

1143
01:16:57.780 --> 01:17:01.000
Sure, Saddam Hussein was awful.
He was a murderer.

1144
01:17:01.117 --> 01:17:05.668
But when Saddam was harming
his own people, that was on him.

1145
01:17:05.788 --> 01:17:07.882
But the American invasion is on us,

1146
01:17:07.998 --> 01:17:11.844
and the people who are harmed
post-invasion is on us.

1147
01:17:11.961 --> 01:17:15.090
It's on our collective national conscience.

1148
01:17:17.216 --> 01:17:20.891
Every person is important,
and every person does matter,

1149
01:17:21.011 --> 01:17:25.562
and we should know exactly
how many people have died

1150
01:17:25.683 --> 01:17:27.481
as a result of this invasion.

1151
01:17:32.648 --> 01:17:34.616
This was a total failure.

1152
01:17:34.733 --> 01:17:38.488
Cost, most of all for the Iraqis,

1153
01:17:38.612 --> 01:17:41.115
lives and property

1154
01:17:41.240 --> 01:17:44.585
and now ten years near anarchy.

1155
01:17:44.702 --> 01:17:47.672
My father fled Iraq
when I was barely a year old.

1156
01:17:47.788 --> 01:17:53.295
I have all my life been an opposition
figure to the Baathist regime.

1157
01:17:55.045 --> 01:18:01.473
But as horrendous and as dark
and as bad and as miserable

1158
01:18:01.594 --> 01:18:05.144
as things got in Iraq under Saddam Hussein,

1159
01:18:05.264 --> 01:18:07.858
incredibly enough,
now they are far, far worse.

1160
01:18:11.478 --> 01:18:15.654
That's what we were there for
on 15th February 2003.

1161
01:18:15.774 --> 01:18:17.401
To stop this from happening.

1162
01:18:17.526 --> 01:18:21.451
My husband is going
to make a short statement.

1163
01:18:21.572 --> 01:18:27.250
He has felt extremely upset
of the injustice of the war,

1164
01:18:27.369 --> 01:18:30.589
and he remains very upset.

1165
01:18:30.706 --> 01:18:33.029
Professor Stephen Hawking.

1166
01:18:40.799 --> 01:18:44.224
The war was based on two lies.

1167
01:18:44.345 --> 01:18:49.897
The first was that we were in danger
from weapons of mass destruction.

1168
01:18:50.017 --> 01:18:54.363
The second was that Iraq
was somehow to blame for 9/11.

1169
01:18:56.398 --> 01:19:01.575
It has been a tragedy for all
the families that have lost members.

1170
01:19:02.071 --> 01:19:07.999
As many as 100,000 may have died,
half of them women and children.

1171
01:19:08.953 --> 01:19:12.753
If that is not a war crime, what is?

1172
01:19:17.544 --> 01:19:19.512
The deaths in Iraq mount daily,

1173
01:19:19.630 --> 01:19:23.726
but we haven't had a running total
or indeed an accurate body count.

1174
01:19:24.843 --> 01:19:27.312
Now we have a shocking figure.

1175
01:19:27.429 --> 01:19:30.524
American and Iraqi
public health experts calculate

1176
01:19:30.641 --> 01:19:35.568
that about 600,000 Iraqis have been
killed as a result of the invasion.

1177
01:19:50.494 --> 01:19:55.751
The number of orphans created
by the Iraq war, 1.25 million.

1178
01:20:07.469 --> 01:20:12.600
Iraqi children suffering from
chronic malnutrition, 28 percent.

1179
01:20:32.161 --> 01:20:37.042
The number of refugees created
by the Iraq war, four million.

1180
01:21:04.234 --> 01:21:07.587
So tonight I'm gonna do one of my slideshows.

1181
01:21:09.698 --> 01:21:11.666
These are actual unstaged photos

1182
01:21:11.784 --> 01:21:16.506
pulled from the files
of the White House photo office.

1183
01:21:19.833 --> 01:21:22.856
Those weapons of mass destruction
have got to be somewhere.

1184
01:21:31.720 --> 01:21:33.017
Nope. No weapons over there.

1185
01:21:48.987 --> 01:21:50.489
Maybe under here.

1186
01:22:19.560 --> 01:22:23.656
Yes, there are consequences of war.

1187
01:22:23.772 --> 01:22:26.616
People will die and some will be innocent,

1188
01:22:26.733 --> 01:22:31.113
and we must live with the consequences
of our actions,

1189
01:22:31.238 --> 01:22:33.661
even the unintended ones.

1190
01:22:33.782 --> 01:22:37.912
I wanted to ask him,
"Have you ever seen what happens

1191
01:22:38.036 --> 01:22:40.915
as somebody rolls a grenade into a tent?"

1192
01:22:41.039 --> 01:22:43.041
"Have you ever imagined what it would be like

1193
01:22:43.167 --> 01:22:47.718
to kneel down beside a soldier
you sent into battle

1194
01:22:47.838 --> 01:22:49.761
and tell him why he's dying?"

1195
01:22:51.091 --> 01:22:58.475
Well, I'd never say that the mass loss
of life of those in the conflict,

1196
01:22:58.599 --> 01:23:03.651
civilians, is worth intervention.

1197
01:23:03.770 --> 01:23:07.400
But I would say that,
were I in the same situation

1198
01:23:07.524 --> 01:23:11.574
with the same information
and the same perspective,

1199
01:23:11.695 --> 01:23:13.242
I would have done the same again.

1200
01:23:13.363 --> 01:23:15.957
I mean, now we know so much more.

1201
01:23:16.074 --> 01:23:21.672
I believed, and I believe,
that the decision was right.

1202
01:23:23.624 --> 01:23:26.343
The Secretary General of the United Nations,

1203
01:23:26.460 --> 01:23:29.964
a man who's always insisted
that America and Britain went to war

1204
01:23:30.088 --> 01:23:33.262
without the legally required authority
of the UN.

1205
01:23:33.383 --> 01:23:35.977
Well, Kofi Annan was asked

1206
01:23:36.094 --> 01:23:42.147
whether he thought that the action was legal.

1207
01:23:42.267 --> 01:23:45.146
I have stated clearly
that it was not in conformity

1208
01:23:45.270 --> 01:23:48.194
with the Security Council,
with the UN Charter.

1209
01:23:48.315 --> 01:23:51.194
And then the next question was,
"So you mean it was illegal?"

1210
01:23:51.318 --> 01:23:53.412
It was illegal?

1211
01:23:53.529 --> 01:23:55.657
Yes, if you wish.

1212
01:23:55.781 --> 01:23:59.126
Which I think the vast majority of
international lawyers in the world

1213
01:23:59.243 --> 01:24:02.588
would say yes, he was right,
and I certainly affirm that it was true.

1214
01:24:02.704 --> 01:24:05.253
Crimes against humanity
were plainly committed.

1215
01:24:05.374 --> 01:24:08.799
War crimes were plainly committed.
There's no issue.

1216
01:24:08.919 --> 01:24:11.763
The photographic and documentary
evidence is overwhelming.

1217
01:24:11.880 --> 01:24:17.057
The holding nations accountable, you know,

1218
01:24:17.177 --> 01:24:22.775
that's for Liberia,
or that's for some small country.

1219
01:24:22.891 --> 01:24:25.189
None of the major heads of state,

1220
01:24:25.310 --> 01:24:29.736
when they engage
in these crimes against humanity,

1221
01:24:29.856 --> 01:24:32.735
are made accountable.

1222
01:24:37.489 --> 01:24:40.914
He's probably the only British
Prime Minister there has ever been

1223
01:24:41.034 --> 01:24:43.583
who cannot appear on the streets of London

1224
01:24:43.704 --> 01:24:47.174
or anywhere in the country
without there being near riots.

1225
01:24:47.291 --> 01:24:53.469
Why are the people who basically
pressed the button and said go

1226
01:24:53.589 --> 01:24:59.096
not accountable for the fact
that they blatantly lied to all of us?

1227
01:24:59.219 --> 01:25:01.692
It was a crime of the century.

1228
01:25:10.022 --> 01:25:13.322
We've campaigned now for an inquiry
for six and a half years.

1229
01:25:13.442 --> 01:25:16.696
Please, Mr. Blair,
give us 15 minutes of your time.

1230
01:25:16.820 --> 01:25:19.824
That's all we're asking.
Our loved ones gave their lives.

1231
01:25:19.948 --> 01:25:22.167
They gave the rest of their time.

1232
01:25:22.284 --> 01:25:24.378
Tony, we want just 15 minutes of yours.

1233
01:25:24.494 --> 01:25:28.294
We have formally sent a letter
through to his office,

1234
01:25:28.415 --> 01:25:32.465
and that request has been totally ignored.

1235
01:25:32.586 --> 01:25:36.432
We, like you, have also experienced
at first hand

1236
01:25:36.548 --> 01:25:40.052
the anger which is still felt
by many people in this country.

1237
01:25:40.177 --> 01:25:42.896
Can I ask whether you have regrets?

1238
01:25:44.014 --> 01:25:48.941
Responsibility, but not a regret
for removing Saddam Hussein.

1239
01:25:49.061 --> 01:25:50.779
- Come on!
- I think he was...

1240
01:25:50.896 --> 01:25:51.988
Be quiet, please.

1241
01:25:55.984 --> 01:25:58.112
They've got to give the appearance
of an inquiry

1242
01:25:58.236 --> 01:26:01.536
because people are so angry
about what happened,

1243
01:26:01.657 --> 01:26:03.876
but it's set up and framed in such a way

1244
01:26:03.992 --> 01:26:05.619
that nobody will be challenged.

1245
01:26:05.744 --> 01:26:08.167
Um, and it's...

1246
01:26:09.623 --> 01:26:13.048
It's what we call a whitewash.

1247
01:26:13.168 --> 01:26:14.795
Blair will go to his death

1248
01:26:14.920 --> 01:26:19.676
with Iraq printed on his heart,
and unforgiven.

1249
01:26:43.615 --> 01:26:46.209
Injustice doesn't go away.
It stays in the psyche.

1250
01:26:46.326 --> 01:26:50.297
It will eventually need to come out.
It will come out. Blood will have blood.

1251
01:26:50.414 --> 01:26:51.836
Sir Roderick...

1252
01:26:51.957 --> 01:26:54.551
There may be things said behind closed doors.

1253
01:26:54.668 --> 01:26:58.718
There may be doors
that are closed silently to Tony Blair.

1254
01:27:00.298 --> 01:27:02.266
But from my point of view, I'm sorry,

1255
01:27:02.384 --> 01:27:04.887
I think he should be, you know, at The Hague.

1256
01:27:05.011 --> 01:27:09.107
He should be tried
for war crimes against society.

1257
01:27:09.224 --> 01:27:11.226
I think he should.

1258
01:27:11.351 --> 01:27:16.357
If it were my choice to have
Dick Cheney, Donald Rumsfeld,

1259
01:27:16.481 --> 01:27:20.281
George Bush go before some kind of tribunal,

1260
01:27:20.402 --> 01:27:22.700
and I had to go with them in order to be...

1261
01:27:22.821 --> 01:27:25.916
in order for that tribunal to be successful

1262
01:27:26.032 --> 01:27:28.205
or to even have a chance for success,

1263
01:27:28.326 --> 01:27:34.299
and that it was also possible I would be
in that conviction, if you will,

1264
01:27:34.416 --> 01:27:36.669
I'd do it in a heartbeat.

1265
01:27:36.793 --> 01:27:40.343
- You feel that strongly about it?
- I feel that strongly about it.

1266
01:27:41.465 --> 01:27:43.888
- Hello, ladies.
- Hello, Mr. Rumsfeld.

1267
01:27:44.009 --> 01:27:45.101
- Hi. How are you.
- Hi.

1268
01:27:45.218 --> 01:27:46.435
- How are you doing?
- Terrific.

1269
01:27:46.553 --> 01:27:48.146
They're all war criminals.

1270
01:27:48.263 --> 01:27:51.233
I saw Donald Rumsfeld
on the street in Washington

1271
01:27:51.349 --> 01:27:53.147
last year one time, and I was aghast.

1272
01:27:53.268 --> 01:27:55.066
"They let you walk around on the street

1273
01:27:55.187 --> 01:27:57.110
like a normal person?
Why aren't you in jail?"

1274
01:27:57.230 --> 01:28:01.781
- What are you here for tonight?
- It's the Correspondents' Dinner.

1275
01:28:01.902 --> 01:28:04.621
OK. Well, enjoy your evening, sir.

1276
01:28:04.738 --> 01:28:06.240
- Thank you very much.
- And madam.

1277
01:28:06.364 --> 01:28:07.581
Thank you.

1278
01:28:10.577 --> 01:28:12.124
- Good evening.
- Hello, folks.

1279
01:28:12.245 --> 01:28:14.293
- Welcome back.
- Thank you.

1280
01:28:14.414 --> 01:28:16.963
- Mr. Secretary.
- War criminal!

1281
01:28:17.083 --> 01:28:20.587
War criminal! War criminal!

1282
01:28:20.712 --> 01:28:24.012
Arrest this man! Arrest the war criminal!

1283
01:28:24.132 --> 01:28:26.226
War criminal!

1284
01:28:26.343 --> 01:28:28.311
- Arrest this man!
- Joyce, Joyce.

1285
01:28:28.428 --> 01:28:30.726
War criminal!

1286
01:28:30.847 --> 01:28:32.849
War criminal!

1287
01:28:32.974 --> 01:28:34.897
You're protecting a man

1288
01:28:35.018 --> 01:28:38.864
who's responsible for the deaths
of millions of Iraqis!

1289
01:28:38.980 --> 01:28:41.199
Shame on you!

1290
01:28:41.316 --> 01:28:46.072
Here comes the war criminal Donald Rumsfeld!

1291
01:28:46.196 --> 01:28:48.449
War criminal!

1292
01:28:48.573 --> 01:28:50.951
He killed people in Iraq!

1293
01:28:51.076 --> 01:28:53.044
War criminal!

1294
01:28:53.161 --> 01:28:58.383
We follow Rumsfeld and Cheney and Bush
and Condoleezza Rice and Karl Rove

1295
01:28:58.500 --> 01:29:02.550
and the people in these think tanks
that lied to get us into the war

1296
01:29:02.671 --> 01:29:05.299
and the journalists who lied
to get us into the war.

1297
01:29:05.423 --> 01:29:08.427
We are constantly following them.

1298
01:29:08.552 --> 01:29:11.601
We feel that we are
the sanity in this country.

1299
01:29:11.721 --> 01:29:15.351
We are the people who are
the conscience of this country,

1300
01:29:15.475 --> 01:29:17.853
and I think history will prove that we are.

1301
01:29:17.978 --> 01:29:22.074
Donald Rumsfeld, war criminal!

1302
01:29:22.190 --> 01:29:24.568
He killed people in Iraq!

1303
01:29:24.693 --> 01:29:28.664
War criminal! There's the war criminal!

1304
01:29:28.780 --> 01:29:32.080
After seeing everything
that the media was feeding us

1305
01:29:32.200 --> 01:29:36.831
and being misled by the Bush administration,

1306
01:29:36.955 --> 01:29:40.004
seven of us young veterans
got together in the same place

1307
01:29:40.125 --> 01:29:42.503
at the Veterans For Peace Conference

1308
01:29:42.627 --> 01:29:46.177
and formed this organization
called Iraq Veterans Against The War.

1309
01:29:48.425 --> 01:29:51.929
We continued to demonstrate
throughout all those years...

1310
01:29:53.722 --> 01:29:56.942
and ever since February 15th 2003,

1311
01:29:57.058 --> 01:30:00.483
we now thought of ourselves
as a global peace movement.

1312
01:30:00.604 --> 01:30:03.357
I never blinked. I never blinked.

1313
01:30:06.610 --> 01:30:09.329
I knew what my morals were.

1314
01:30:09.446 --> 01:30:12.245
I knew that the war was wrong,

1315
01:30:12.365 --> 01:30:18.793
and every ounce of my being compelled me
to continue protesting that war.

1316
01:30:19.789 --> 01:30:23.384
- No NATO, no war!
- No NATO, no war!

1317
01:30:23.501 --> 01:30:27.506
- We don't work for you no more.
- We don't work for you no more.

1318
01:30:27.631 --> 01:30:29.725
My name is Jason Hurd.

1319
01:30:29.841 --> 01:30:33.095
I spent ten years in the United States
Army as a combat medic.

1320
01:30:33.219 --> 01:30:36.143
I deployed to Baghdad in 2004.

1321
01:30:36.264 --> 01:30:39.564
I'm here to return my Global
War On Terrorism service medal

1322
01:30:39.684 --> 01:30:44.155
in solidarity with the people of Iraq
and the people of Afghanistan.

1323
01:30:44.272 --> 01:30:46.570
These were lies. I'm giving them back.

1324
01:30:52.238 --> 01:30:56.664
I'm one of 40,000 people that left
the United States Armed Forces

1325
01:30:56.785 --> 01:30:59.629
because this is a lie!

1326
01:31:07.253 --> 01:31:10.427
Our enemies are not 7,000 miles from home.

1327
01:31:10.548 --> 01:31:13.768
They sit in boardrooms. They are CEOs.

1328
01:31:13.885 --> 01:31:17.856
They are the millionaires and
billionaires who control this planet,

1329
01:31:17.973 --> 01:31:19.941
and we've had enough of it.

1330
01:31:20.058 --> 01:31:22.060
So they can take their medals back.

1331
01:31:41.496 --> 01:31:45.091
Egypt is tasting
its own dose of people power.

1332
01:31:45.208 --> 01:31:47.461
Such defiance here is rare

1333
01:31:47.585 --> 01:31:49.758
and could mark the beginning
of something bigger.

1334
01:31:49.879 --> 01:31:53.258
There is a big myth both in Egypt,
in the Arab world,

1335
01:31:53.383 --> 01:31:55.010
as well as the West

1336
01:31:55.135 --> 01:31:58.435
that this revolution in Egypt
broke out of the blue.

1337
01:31:58.555 --> 01:32:00.557
Some people deliberately tried to call it

1338
01:32:00.682 --> 01:32:02.855
a Facebook revolution or a Twitter revolution

1339
01:32:02.976 --> 01:32:06.401
or an internet revolution
or even a youth revolution,

1340
01:32:06.521 --> 01:32:12.824
uh, in order, more or less, to dismiss
the fact that this revolution

1341
01:32:12.944 --> 01:32:16.539
has been in the making
for at least ten years.

1342
01:32:16.656 --> 01:32:18.829
The demonstrations did not stop

1343
01:32:18.950 --> 01:32:21.328
after the invasion of Iraq. They went on.

1344
01:32:25.206 --> 01:32:29.177
True credit goes to these people
who were out there

1345
01:32:29.294 --> 01:32:33.344
ten years before it would actually
flower into something.

1346
01:32:33.465 --> 01:32:39.143
So God bless. That's what...
That's what revolutions come from.

1347
01:32:44.642 --> 01:32:49.944
11th February at 6:00 p.m.,
my mother told me, "Open the TV."

1348
01:32:59.991 --> 01:33:03.871
This huge, you know, headline
was spread across the screen.

1349
01:33:05.705 --> 01:33:08.003
And I was screaming and jumping up and down

1350
01:33:08.124 --> 01:33:10.593
and saying, "He's gone.
He's stepped down. He's stepped down."

1351
01:33:10.710 --> 01:33:13.884
I don't know. I lost control.

1352
01:33:16.966 --> 01:33:20.516
The first thing people of my generation
when they met,

1353
01:33:20.637 --> 01:33:24.232
obviously, you know, you get
the hugs and kisses and so on.

1354
01:33:24.349 --> 01:33:28.650
But the first words we would say is
"We lived to see it."

1355
01:33:36.986 --> 01:33:38.829
No matter what happens,

1356
01:33:38.947 --> 01:33:44.670
even if we end up in the short term
having an authoritarian military regime,

1357
01:33:44.786 --> 01:33:48.711
I think we realize that nobody
can do to us what used to be done.

1358
01:33:48.832 --> 01:33:50.675
Nobody can boss us around anymore.

1359
01:33:50.792 --> 01:33:54.092
No one can enslave us anymore
the way Mubarak did,

1360
01:33:54.212 --> 01:33:57.136
and eventually I think we're going
to get rid of the military.

1361
01:34:09.185 --> 01:34:11.404
This is one of the fruits
of the anti-war movement,

1362
01:34:11.521 --> 01:34:13.819
of the global anti-war movement, of course.

1363
01:34:31.791 --> 01:34:35.796
This, according to a UK intelligence
document released today,

1364
01:34:35.920 --> 01:34:39.891
was a chemical attack which killed
more than 300 people in Damascus.

1365
01:34:40.008 --> 01:34:44.434
This kind of attack threatens
our national security interests.

1366
01:34:44.554 --> 01:34:47.398
I don't believe we can let that stand.

1367
01:34:47.515 --> 01:34:52.487
Every war that comes along,
it's the same damn language.

1368
01:34:52.604 --> 01:34:56.654
"They are horrible. They are not human."

1369
01:34:56.774 --> 01:35:00.574
"They cannot be trusted.
We cannot negotiate with them."

1370
01:35:00.695 --> 01:35:02.823
How many times and how many wars?

1371
01:35:02.947 --> 01:35:07.373
Take out Vietnam, put in Afghanistan.
Take out Afghanistan, put in Iran.

1372
01:35:07.493 --> 01:35:10.542
Take out communism, put in terrorism.
The same thing.

1373
01:35:10.663 --> 01:35:15.294
Hands off Syria! Hands off Syria!
Hands off Syria! Hands off Syria!

1374
01:35:15.418 --> 01:35:18.016
It was like this feeling that...

1375
01:35:18.588 --> 01:35:21.808
Take a deep breath and go full steam ahead.

1376
01:35:21.925 --> 01:35:27.182
Once again we are out on the streets
to stop yet another war.

1377
01:35:27.305 --> 01:35:30.525
The controversial decision
to go to war in Iraq,

1378
01:35:30.642 --> 01:35:32.736
made here ten years ago,

1379
01:35:32.852 --> 01:35:34.854
has had a profound influence
over this debate.

1380
01:35:34.979 --> 01:35:38.529
Overwhelmingly, the people of Britain
are telling us

1381
01:35:38.650 --> 01:35:41.119
no to immediate action, no to strikes.

1382
01:35:41.236 --> 01:35:43.409
MPs are doing their job.

1383
01:35:43.529 --> 01:35:45.998
They are listening to what the public want.

1384
01:35:46.115 --> 01:35:52.748
All of our constituents are scarred
by the Iraq and Afghanistan experience.

1385
01:35:52.872 --> 01:35:56.126
I cannot sit in this House
and be duped again.

1386
01:35:56.251 --> 01:36:01.132
The well of public opinion was well
and truly poisoned by the Iraq episode,

1387
01:36:01.256 --> 01:36:04.760
and we need to understand
the public skepticism.

1388
01:36:04.884 --> 01:36:07.763
The MPs in Parliament should do

1389
01:36:07.887 --> 01:36:11.391
what not enough of them
had the guts to do ten years ago

1390
01:36:11.516 --> 01:36:13.109
and vote against!

1391
01:36:18.356 --> 01:36:21.576
Order, order.

1392
01:36:23.027 --> 01:36:24.995
Recite.

1393
01:36:25.113 --> 01:36:31.746
The ayes to the right 272,
the noes to the left 285.

1394
01:36:35.957 --> 01:36:39.962
The House of Commons
sees itself in a new light today

1395
01:36:40.086 --> 01:36:44.387
after last night's historic defeat
for a British Prime Minister.

1396
01:36:51.222 --> 01:36:53.350
It is clear to me
that the British Parliament,

1397
01:36:53.474 --> 01:36:55.647
reflecting the views of the British people,

1398
01:36:55.768 --> 01:36:58.988
does not want to see British military action.

1399
01:36:59.105 --> 01:37:01.483
I get that,
and the Government will act accordingly.

1400
01:37:03.192 --> 01:37:07.618
What? The retrieval of democracy in the UK?

1401
01:37:07.739 --> 01:37:08.831
This is one of the occasions

1402
01:37:08.948 --> 01:37:11.292
when something sensational
has happened in Parliament.

1403
01:37:11.409 --> 01:37:12.706
- Parliament matters.
- Yeah.

1404
01:37:12.827 --> 01:37:15.706
As of now, I think we would all agree

1405
01:37:15.830 --> 01:37:18.379
that we're in some kind of state
of social revolution.

1406
01:37:18.499 --> 01:37:20.092
Quiet, quiet revolution.

1407
01:37:30.136 --> 01:37:32.764
MPs have, as you say, listened to the public,

1408
01:37:32.889 --> 01:37:36.314
and the public was so appalled
by what happened

1409
01:37:36.434 --> 01:37:40.234
under the Prime Ministership
of Tony Blair and the Iraq War

1410
01:37:40.355 --> 01:37:43.074
and the dodgy dossier
that actually they say, "No more."

1411
01:37:44.776 --> 01:37:47.620
And I have been on this platform
over many years,

1412
01:37:47.737 --> 01:37:51.116
from the time of Suez onwards, over 50 years,

1413
01:37:51.240 --> 01:37:55.120
and usually we've found
that we have lost a battle.

1414
01:37:55.244 --> 01:37:57.121
But what we must remember

1415
01:37:57.246 --> 01:38:00.375
is that all the campaigns
we've been engaged in

1416
01:38:00.500 --> 01:38:05.902
have culminated in the decision
taken by Parliament last week.

1417
01:38:07.548 --> 01:38:09.425
It's been without any doubt

1418
01:38:09.550 --> 01:38:13.680
the most powerful political campaign
in my lifetime.

1419
01:38:15.765 --> 01:38:18.393
We said, "Wow, if the British Parliament

1420
01:38:18.518 --> 01:38:21.692
is actually listening to the British people,

1421
01:38:21.813 --> 01:38:27.661
then why can't the US Congress
listen to the American people?"

1422
01:38:28.986 --> 01:38:31.239
Protestors have been outside the White House

1423
01:38:31.364 --> 01:38:33.287
almost constantly.

1424
01:38:34.534 --> 01:38:38.630
The President has spent days huddled
with his advisors at the White House,

1425
01:38:38.746 --> 01:38:40.794
but when he emerged from the Oval Office,

1426
01:38:40.915 --> 01:38:42.838
no one expected this outcome.

1427
01:38:42.959 --> 01:38:47.260
Many people have advised against
taking this decision to Congress.

1428
01:38:47.380 --> 01:38:49.178
And undoubtedly they were impacted

1429
01:38:49.298 --> 01:38:51.892
by what we saw happen
in the United Kingdom this week

1430
01:38:52.009 --> 01:38:54.262
when the Parliament of our closest ally

1431
01:38:54.387 --> 01:38:56.856
failed to pass a resolution
with a similar goal.

1432
01:38:56.973 --> 01:39:00.147
Then came the decision that borrowed
from David Cameron's playbook.

1433
01:39:00.268 --> 01:39:04.489
I will seek authorization
for the use of force

1434
01:39:04.605 --> 01:39:07.950
from the American people's
representatives in Congress.

1435
01:39:13.156 --> 01:39:16.330
Now we must stand up and act.

1436
01:39:16.451 --> 01:39:19.303
Thank you, Mr. Secretary.

1437
01:39:20.288 --> 01:39:22.336
The Committee will be in order.

1438
01:39:22.457 --> 01:39:25.085
The Committee will be in order.

1439
01:39:25.209 --> 01:39:28.804
- We don't want another war!
- May I ask the police to escort her?

1440
01:39:28.921 --> 01:39:32.050
Wait a minute! Nobody wants this war!

1441
01:39:33.050 --> 01:39:35.678
Launching cruise missiles means another war!

1442
01:39:35.803 --> 01:39:38.647
- Easy with her.
- The American people do not want this!

1443
01:39:38.764 --> 01:39:42.189
You know, the first time I testified
before this Committee

1444
01:39:42.310 --> 01:39:44.608
when I was 27 years old,

1445
01:39:44.729 --> 01:39:49.735
I had feelings very similar
to that protestor.

1446
01:39:49.859 --> 01:39:51.532
That is exactly why it is so important

1447
01:39:51.652 --> 01:39:56.328
that the Congress itself will act
representing the American people.

1448
01:39:56.449 --> 01:39:58.918
It was like a magical moment.

1449
01:39:59.035 --> 01:40:01.413
It was one of the rare moments

1450
01:40:01.537 --> 01:40:05.167
that our Congress people
were actually listening to us.

1451
01:40:05.291 --> 01:40:08.010
And we won. That was the extraordinary part.

1452
01:40:08.127 --> 01:40:09.595
We're finished.

1453
01:40:12.840 --> 01:40:16.219
History will record this vote on Syria

1454
01:40:16.344 --> 01:40:19.769
as the time that the American people,

1455
01:40:19.889 --> 01:40:23.439
thanks in large part to the British people,

1456
01:40:23.559 --> 01:40:29.066
were able to stop the empire
from going to war before it started.

1457
01:40:30.983 --> 01:40:32.826
What everybody now knows

1458
01:40:32.944 --> 01:40:35.868
is that these deranged lefties
were absolutely right,

1459
01:40:35.988 --> 01:40:40.209
that the Stop The War Coalition
was incredibly right,

1460
01:40:40.326 --> 01:40:45.298
whereas all the people with MI6,
the CIA, the British Foreign Office,

1461
01:40:45.414 --> 01:40:48.133
the Prime Minister, the Conservative Party,

1462
01:40:48.251 --> 01:40:51.346
the mainstream Labour Party, were wrong,

1463
01:40:51.462 --> 01:40:53.135
and that's quite something.

1464
01:40:53.256 --> 01:40:58.183
This handful of people
have kept this movement going,

1465
01:40:58.302 --> 01:41:00.225
and the result is good

1466
01:41:00.346 --> 01:41:03.350
because it maintains an anti-war presence

1467
01:41:03.474 --> 01:41:05.602
in British political culture.

1468
01:41:05.726 --> 01:41:12.200
If you keep coming back, at some point
you will make the change.

1469
01:41:12.316 --> 01:41:15.570
If a million people come out
on the street in the future,

1470
01:41:15.695 --> 01:41:18.323
then what government is going
to say they're wrong now?

1471
01:41:18.447 --> 01:41:24.454
When the last time the public expressed
the opposition that way,

1472
01:41:24.579 --> 01:41:27.958
namely 15th February 2003,

1473
01:41:28.082 --> 01:41:30.835
history said that the people
on the street were right

1474
01:41:30.960 --> 01:41:33.338
and not the people in the government.

1475
01:41:42.138 --> 01:41:44.015
I think it shaped people.

1476
01:41:44.140 --> 01:41:48.987
The fact of that march
has shaped political debate

1477
01:41:49.103 --> 01:41:51.902
and the public understanding ever since.

1478
01:41:58.946 --> 01:42:04.043
The Iraq War marks a turn
in history in this respect,

1479
01:42:04.160 --> 01:42:06.003
and I'm optimistic about it.

1480
01:42:06.120 --> 01:42:11.126
I think there will be less use
of military action in the future.

1481
01:43:47.722 --> 01:43:53.149
They are few, and we are many,
and if we come together, we are a force.

1482
01:43:58.983 --> 01:44:01.702
There are two forces at work always.

1483
01:44:01.819 --> 01:44:05.699
A hatred of injustice, which makes you angry,

1484
01:44:05.823 --> 01:44:09.956
and a belief you can make a better world,
which makes you optimistic.

1485
01:44:10.077 --> 01:44:13.581
And anger and optimism coming together

1486
01:44:13.706 --> 01:44:15.879
are a very powerful force.
